window.CET6_BANKS.push({"id": "2025-06-1", "source": "2025年06月真题·第1套", "writing": {"prompt": "Directions: For this part, you are allowed 30 minutes to write an essay that begins with the sentence \"As requirements for job applications are getting increasingly higher, college students ought to be better prepared for their future career.\" You can make comments, cite examples or use your personal experiences to develop your essay. You should write at least 150 words but no more than 200 words. You should copy the sentence given in quotes at the beginning of your essay.", "sample": "As requirements for job applications are getting increasingly higher, college students ought to be better\nprepared for their future career. In an era when employers demand not only a diploma but also practical\ncompetence, waiting until graduation to think about work is simply too late.\nThere are concrete steps every student can take. To begin with, obtaining relevant certificates and mastering\nprofessional skills builds a solid foundation. Last summer, for instance, I taught myself data analysis and\nearned a certificate, which later helped me stand out in an internship interview. What is more, internships offer\nirreplaceable experience: by working in a real company, students learn how a workplace actually operates and\ndiscover which career truly suits them. Such preparation turns abstract knowledge into employable ability.\nIn conclusion, the fiercer competition becomes, the more valuable early preparation is. Therefore, college\nstudents should seize every chance to enrich their skills and experience, so that when opportunity knocks, they\nare ready to open the door."}, "translation": {"prompt": "天宫空间站(Tiangong Space Station)是中国首个太空实验室,拥有110多立方米使用空间,可驻留三名宇航员,在距地球表面400-450公里的轨道上运行。天宫空间站已实施180多个科学研究与应用项目,涉及空间生命科学、太空医学、空间材料科学等领域。天宫空间站的研究成果在我国得到了广泛应用,产生了显著的经济效益。例如,太空育种创造的直接经济效益高达数千亿元。这不仅标志中国在航天技术上取得了巨大进步,也表明中国为全球的太空研究和应用做出了重大贡献。", "sample": "The Tiangong Space Station, China's first space laboratory, boasts over 110 cubic meters of usable space and is able to accommodate three astronauts, orbiting at an altitude of 400 to 450 kilometers above the Earth's surface. The station has carried out more than 180 scientific research and application projects, covering such fields as space life science, space medicine, and space materials science. The research achievements of the station have been widely applied in China and have generated remarkable economic benefits. For example, the direct economic benefits created by space breeding reach hundreds of billions of yuan. This not only marks China's tremendous progress in aerospace technology, but also demonstrates that China has made significant contributions to global space research and application."}, "listeningUrl": "https://english-exam.lazynote.cn/cet6/paper/2025-06-1/?f=w", "clozePassage": "In her second year, Charlene Duong learned of the use of poisonous, synthetic pesticides on her college campus. Shocked but not surprised, she knew she had to do something. Along with a couple of classmates, Charlene did a quick web search and discovered a small but growing movement led by the organization, Herbicide-Free Campus (HFC), to rid college campuses of artificial herbicides(灭草剂). They were  __26__  .\nLike many, Charlene experiences climate anxiety—a  __27__  fear of a climate catastrophe—and was, at the time, looking for an  __28__  . When she discovered the HFC movement, she said she felt she \"had found a specific area to focus on that still fit into the larger picture of fighting for a healthier, safer, cleaner  __29__  for all.\"\nToxic herbicide use in university land care is not unique. Most institutions of higher education rely on synthetic pesticides and fertilizers to achieve  __30__  goals. Having a \"beautiful\" campus means green and perfectly maintained lawns along with flower beds and paved sidewalks. But these  __31__  managed campuses can come at a cost: increased cancer risk,  __32__  waterways, poisoned wildlife and lifeless soil.\nPesticide use on college campuses also contributes to our global climate crisis. The use of chemicals to get rid of insects or unwanted plant life can increase indirect  __33__  as they can include petroleum-based ingredients. Pesticide use also decreases the life in soil,  __34__  the ability of soils to absorb carbon or retain water and thus reducing campuses' ability to recover quickly from climate-related extreme weather events like droughts and floods.\nInstead of using toxic chemicals, students working with HFC help out with  __35__  the campus grounds.\n\"This work reminds me to be in the present moment as I play my role in reducing herbicide use and keeping my campus safe and healthy,\" says Charlene.", "matchIntro": "Why Your Library Is the Most Important Place in Town", "matchParagraphs": [{"label": "A", "text": "Librarians know the value of their community services, and their patrons appreciate their importance as well. But in an increasingly digital world, we see the role of libraries as community and cultural centers at times undervalued. When shrinking municipal budgets combine with the nonstop technological revolution, public library services that focus on building communities face-to-face, inspiring and educating patrons about art, literature, and music, and helping patrons engage in civil discourse can seem old-fashioned. But it is precisely those shrinking budgets and the assault of technologically mediated life that make public libraries' cultural and community offerings more important than ever."}, {"label": "B", "text": "Many people point out the value public libraries bring to their communities. More than just books and banks of computers, libraries are still places where individuals gather to explore, interact, and imagine. Some of the specific ways in which libraries add value to our communities and serve as cultural centers for our patrons are community builders, centers for the arts, and champions of youth. Libraries serve in these capacities and are more than just about information."}, {"label": "C", "text": "As community builders libraries are engaged in incredible work. From tiny public libraries to huge city institutions with multiple branches, libraries across the United States are building community and supporting local culture in exciting ways. These are inspiring and hopefully will encourage librarians interested in community services and cultural outreach to make connections with each other, share ideas, and build partnerships. Supporters of libraries as community builders claim that unless you are out there changing neighborhoods, you are not completing the work you are to do. Strengthening neighborhoods and championing the cultural lives of communities are big responsibilities."}, {"label": "D", "text": "Place-based economic development stresses the importance of offering attractive, functional, and community-based places, such as libraries, in town squares and depressed neighborhoods. Like a major department store in a mall, libraries attract large numbers of people, creating economic opportunities for numerous businesses and organizations in the surrounding area. Large cities, medium-sized ones, and even small towns have successfully transformed their libraries into the hubs(枢纽) of vibrant neighborhoods."}, {"label": "E", "text": "As key municipal agencies, and focal points for community education, libraries are major players in creating livable, environmentally friendly cities and towns. The Urban Libraries Council released a report detailing the unique ways in which libraries can further sustainability at the local level. Beyond ensuring that library construction projects consider environmental impact, libraries can take a lead in supporting local foods and craftsmen, like the Peabody Institute Library's partnering with local businesses to pioneer a farmers' market in their courtyard, or the Richmond Public Library's seed lending library which \"nurtures locally-adapted plant varieties, and fosters community resilience(韧性), self-reliance and a culture of sharing.\""}, {"label": "F", "text": "Archives preserve historic artifacts, oral histories, digital history projects, and scholarly writings relevant to the community, including minority groups. Communities lucky enough to have archivists have a great advantage when it comes to organizing historical records and artifacts. An organized archive is a place where people can research their ancestry and immigration history, do environmental research, and more. An archivist is an advocate for preservation who, among other things, coordinates the restoration of maps and paintings, the digitization of vital records, and the creation of oral history projects. With projects like the Massachussets Memories Road Show and the Veterans History Project, evidence of the importance of archives is everywhere."}, {"label": "G", "text": "In the words of Robert Putnam, \"People may go to the library looking mainly for information, but they find each other there.\" New moms connect at baby story-times; elderly people, often facing difficult life transitions, attend events and find that they make new friends; teenagers meet up in libraries' teen spaces after school; and readers discuss current events in the periodicals room. In libraries, community-building connections are happening all the time."}, {"label": "H", "text": "As Keith Richards said, \"The public library is the great equalizer.\" Despite the rising costs of concert and theater tickets, public library events (including concerts, author visits, and gallery displays) are often offered free of charge, enabling people of any income level to attend. In addition, library book groups allow people to explore and discuss the literary arts, and the Great Stories Club introduces at-risk youth to literature. The best part: it's all free and open to the public."}, {"label": "I", "text": "In a time when education is increasingly expensive, public libraries provide information and educational opportunities free for all people, regardless of their socio-economic status. Offered by libraries across the country, American Library Association's Let's Talk about It programs are wonderful examples of scholar-facilitated learning opportunities in libraries. In addition, many libraries present classes and discussion programs, and some even provide online continuing education courses such as the Universal Class database."}, {"label": "J", "text": "Librarians know that patrons aren't just information consumers, they're information producers. Patrons use the library to gain knowledge in order to create their own new and independent works. Increasing numbers of libraries provide spaces and services that meet the needs of people who want to learn how to edit Wikipedia, set up blogs or podcasts, create their own magazines, and so much more. Many libraries offer art or writing workshops and groups, and some provide music practice rooms for patrons. Programs like ImaginOn in Charlotte, North Carolina, provide exciting models that take community partnership, creativity, and creation to a new level."}, {"label": "K", "text": "The decline of civil discourse stems in part from the fact that it is so easy for people to watch news about, buy products from, and engage—in both the virtual and real worlds—only with those of similar backgrounds and ideologies. Public libraries, through such programs as The Human Library and Socrates Café, can help build small communities of difference that encourage people to interact with and learn from each other through dialogue. By both actively promoting civil discourse through these programs, and modeling and upholding the principles of free inquiry and expression for all, libraries help individuals rediscover the importance of and increased need for civil discourse in American life."}, {"label": "L", "text": "Free tutoring, homework help programs, and summer reading programs for kids and teens help bridge the economic divide that impacts students' academic performance. The cost of hiring a private tutor is well beyond what many library patrons can afford, so libraries offer homework help and tutoring online, by phone, in person, and even through social media and homework apps. Annual summer reading programs also have a positive impact on student performance and, according to a 2010 study conducted by Dominican University's Graduate School of Library and Information Science, students' reading skills get a boost from these popular nationwide events."}, {"label": "M", "text": "Through library collections, programs, and physical spaces, children learn to share, to be engaged in their communities, to participate in the arts, and to explore their immediate world and the world at large. There are surely endless examples of innovative library services for children, including the Middle Country Public Library's Nature Explorium, which engages children in learning about the natural world."}, {"label": "N", "text": "These examples are just a few of the many amazing things that public libraries around the United States (and the world) are doing to build and maintain strong community connections. We encourage you to try some of these ideas in your own libraries, and we hope that these ideas will help you be better able to convince your community leaders of the important role that public libraries play in communities large and small."}], "readingPassages": {"46": "Nationally, one in six children miss 15 or more days of school in a year. Education officials have deplored all this missed instruction.\nThese chronically absent students suffer academically because of all the classroom instruction they miss out on. In 2015, the U.S. secretary of education responded to this crisis, urging communities to support every student to attend every day and be successful in school. His open letter stated that missing 10% of school days in a year for any reason—excused or unexcused—is a primary cause of low academic achievement.\nWorrying about whether children attend school makes sense. After all, if students don't show up, teachers can't teach them.\nBut what if America's attendance crisis is about much more than students missing class? What if, instead, it is a reflection of family and community crises these students face—such as being ejected from the family apartment, fearing for their safety in their neighborhood or suffering an illness?\nAs social scientists we investigated how excused and unexcused absences relate to children's academic achievement.\nWe find that absences excused by a parent do little to harm children's learning. In fact, children with no unexcused absences—but 15 to 18 excused absences—have test scores equal to their peers who have no absences.\nMeanwhile, the average child with even just one unexcused absence does much worse academically than peers with none.\nWe believe unexcused absence is a strong signal of the many challenges children and families face, including economic and medical hardships. Unexcused absences can be a powerful signal of how those out-of-school challenges affect children's academic progress.\nOur evidence suggests unexcused absences are problematic, but for a different reason than people often think. Absence from school, and especially unexcused absence, matters mainly as a signal of many crises children and their families may be facing. It matters less as a cause of lower student achievement due to missed instruction.\nHow we choose to think of school absences matters for educational policy. School attendance policies typically hold schools and families accountable for the days children miss, regardless of whether they were excused or unexcused absences.\nThese policies assume that missing school for any reason harms children academically because they are missing classroom instruction. They also assume that schools will be able to effectively intervene by reducing student absences. We find neither to be the case.\nAs a result, these attendance policies end up disproportionately punishing families dealing with out-of-school crises in their lives and pressuring schools who serve them to get students to school more often.\nWe instead suggest using unexcused absence from school as a signal to channel resources to the children and families who need them most.", "47": "Nationally, one in six children miss 15 or more days of school in a year. Education officials have deplored all this missed instruction.\nThese chronically absent students suffer academically because of all the classroom instruction they miss out on. In 2015, the U.S. secretary of education responded to this crisis, urging communities to support every student to attend every day and be successful in school. His open letter stated that missing 10% of school days in a year for any reason—excused or unexcused—is a primary cause of low academic achievement.\nWorrying about whether children attend school makes sense. After all, if students don't show up, teachers can't teach them.\nBut what if America's attendance crisis is about much more than students missing class? What if, instead, it is a reflection of family and community crises these students face—such as being ejected from the family apartment, fearing for their safety in their neighborhood or suffering an illness?\nAs social scientists we investigated how excused and unexcused absences relate to children's academic achievement.\nWe find that absences excused by a parent do little to harm children's learning. In fact, children with no unexcused absences—but 15 to 18 excused absences—have test scores equal to their peers who have no absences.\nMeanwhile, the average child with even just one unexcused absence does much worse academically than peers with none.\nWe believe unexcused absence is a strong signal of the many challenges children and families face, including economic and medical hardships. Unexcused absences can be a powerful signal of how those out-of-school challenges affect children's academic progress.\nOur evidence suggests unexcused absences are problematic, but for a different reason than people often think. Absence from school, and especially unexcused absence, matters mainly as a signal of many crises children and their families may be facing. It matters less as a cause of lower student achievement due to missed instruction.\nHow we choose to think of school absences matters for educational policy. School attendance policies typically hold schools and families accountable for the days children miss, regardless of whether they were excused or unexcused absences.\nThese policies assume that missing school for any reason harms children academically because they are missing classroom instruction. They also assume that schools will be able to effectively intervene by reducing student absences. We find neither to be the case.\nAs a result, these attendance policies end up disproportionately punishing families dealing with out-of-school crises in their lives and pressuring schools who serve them to get students to school more often.\nWe instead suggest using unexcused absence from school as a signal to channel resources to the children and families who need them most.", "48": "Nationally, one in six children miss 15 or more days of school in a year. Education officials have deplored all this missed instruction.\nThese chronically absent students suffer academically because of all the classroom instruction they miss out on. In 2015, the U.S. secretary of education responded to this crisis, urging communities to support every student to attend every day and be successful in school. His open letter stated that missing 10% of school days in a year for any reason—excused or unexcused—is a primary cause of low academic achievement.\nWorrying about whether children attend school makes sense. After all, if students don't show up, teachers can't teach them.\nBut what if America's attendance crisis is about much more than students missing class? What if, instead, it is a reflection of family and community crises these students face—such as being ejected from the family apartment, fearing for their safety in their neighborhood or suffering an illness?\nAs social scientists we investigated how excused and unexcused absences relate to children's academic achievement.\nWe find that absences excused by a parent do little to harm children's learning. In fact, children with no unexcused absences—but 15 to 18 excused absences—have test scores equal to their peers who have no absences.\nMeanwhile, the average child with even just one unexcused absence does much worse academically than peers with none.\nWe believe unexcused absence is a strong signal of the many challenges children and families face, including economic and medical hardships. Unexcused absences can be a powerful signal of how those out-of-school challenges affect children's academic progress.\nOur evidence suggests unexcused absences are problematic, but for a different reason than people often think. Absence from school, and especially unexcused absence, matters mainly as a signal of many crises children and their families may be facing. It matters less as a cause of lower student achievement due to missed instruction.\nHow we choose to think of school absences matters for educational policy. School attendance policies typically hold schools and families accountable for the days children miss, regardless of whether they were excused or unexcused absences.\nThese policies assume that missing school for any reason harms children academically because they are missing classroom instruction. They also assume that schools will be able to effectively intervene by reducing student absences. We find neither to be the case.\nAs a result, these attendance policies end up disproportionately punishing families dealing with out-of-school crises in their lives and pressuring schools who serve them to get students to school more often.\nWe instead suggest using unexcused absence from school as a signal to channel resources to the children and families who need them most.", "49": "Nationally, one in six children miss 15 or more days of school in a year. Education officials have deplored all this missed instruction.\nThese chronically absent students suffer academically because of all the classroom instruction they miss out on. In 2015, the U.S. secretary of education responded to this crisis, urging communities to support every student to attend every day and be successful in school. His open letter stated that missing 10% of school days in a year for any reason—excused or unexcused—is a primary cause of low academic achievement.\nWorrying about whether children attend school makes sense. After all, if students don't show up, teachers can't teach them.\nBut what if America's attendance crisis is about much more than students missing class? What if, instead, it is a reflection of family and community crises these students face—such as being ejected from the family apartment, fearing for their safety in their neighborhood or suffering an illness?\nAs social scientists we investigated how excused and unexcused absences relate to children's academic achievement.\nWe find that absences excused by a parent do little to harm children's learning. In fact, children with no unexcused absences—but 15 to 18 excused absences—have test scores equal to their peers who have no absences.\nMeanwhile, the average child with even just one unexcused absence does much worse academically than peers with none.\nWe believe unexcused absence is a strong signal of the many challenges children and families face, including economic and medical hardships. Unexcused absences can be a powerful signal of how those out-of-school challenges affect children's academic progress.\nOur evidence suggests unexcused absences are problematic, but for a different reason than people often think. Absence from school, and especially unexcused absence, matters mainly as a signal of many crises children and their families may be facing. It matters less as a cause of lower student achievement due to missed instruction.\nHow we choose to think of school absences matters for educational policy. School attendance policies typically hold schools and families accountable for the days children miss, regardless of whether they were excused or unexcused absences.\nThese policies assume that missing school for any reason harms children academically because they are missing classroom instruction. They also assume that schools will be able to effectively intervene by reducing student absences. We find neither to be the case.\nAs a result, these attendance policies end up disproportionately punishing families dealing with out-of-school crises in their lives and pressuring schools who serve them to get students to school more often.\nWe instead suggest using unexcused absence from school as a signal to channel resources to the children and families who need them most.", "50": "Nationally, one in six children miss 15 or more days of school in a year. Education officials have deplored all this missed instruction.\nThese chronically absent students suffer academically because of all the classroom instruction they miss out on. In 2015, the U.S. secretary of education responded to this crisis, urging communities to support every student to attend every day and be successful in school. His open letter stated that missing 10% of school days in a year for any reason—excused or unexcused—is a primary cause of low academic achievement.\nWorrying about whether children attend school makes sense. After all, if students don't show up, teachers can't teach them.\nBut what if America's attendance crisis is about much more than students missing class? What if, instead, it is a reflection of family and community crises these students face—such as being ejected from the family apartment, fearing for their safety in their neighborhood or suffering an illness?\nAs social scientists we investigated how excused and unexcused absences relate to children's academic achievement.\nWe find that absences excused by a parent do little to harm children's learning. In fact, children with no unexcused absences—but 15 to 18 excused absences—have test scores equal to their peers who have no absences.\nMeanwhile, the average child with even just one unexcused absence does much worse academically than peers with none.\nWe believe unexcused absence is a strong signal of the many challenges children and families face, including economic and medical hardships. Unexcused absences can be a powerful signal of how those out-of-school challenges affect children's academic progress.\nOur evidence suggests unexcused absences are problematic, but for a different reason than people often think. Absence from school, and especially unexcused absence, matters mainly as a signal of many crises children and their families may be facing. It matters less as a cause of lower student achievement due to missed instruction.\nHow we choose to think of school absences matters for educational policy. School attendance policies typically hold schools and families accountable for the days children miss, regardless of whether they were excused or unexcused absences.\nThese policies assume that missing school for any reason harms children academically because they are missing classroom instruction. They also assume that schools will be able to effectively intervene by reducing student absences. We find neither to be the case.\nAs a result, these attendance policies end up disproportionately punishing families dealing with out-of-school crises in their lives and pressuring schools who serve them to get students to school more often.\nWe instead suggest using unexcused absence from school as a signal to channel resources to the children and families who need them most.", "51": "After earning a bachelor's degree, I was determined to do what I love. I headed straight to graduate school to investigate the social problems that fascinated me.\nFor almost a decade, I told everyone I encountered that they should do the same. \"Follow your passion,\" I counseled. \"You can figure out the employment stuff later.\"\nIt wasn't until I began to research this widely accepted career advice that I understood how problematic it really was.\nAs a sociologist, I interviewed college students and professional workers to learn what it really meant to pursue their dreams, which I will refer to here as the passion principle. I was stunned by what I found out about this principle in the research for my new book.\nSurveys show the American public has long held the passion principle in high regard as a career decision-making priority. And its popularity is even stronger among those facing job instability.\nAdvocates of the passion principle found it compelling because they believed that following one's passion can provide workers with both the motivation necessary to work hard and a place to find fulfillment.\nYet, what I found is that following one's passion does not necessarily lead to fulfillment, but is one of the most powerful cultural forces perpetuating overwork. I also found that promoting the pursuit of one's passion helps perpetuate social inequalities due to the fact that not everyone has the same economic resources to allow them to pursue their passion with ease.\nWhile the passion principle is broadly popular, not everyone has the necessary resources to turn their passion into a stable, good-paying job. Passion-seekers from wealthy families are better able to wait until a job they are passionate about comes along without worrying about student loans in the meantime. They are also better situated to take unpaid internships to get their foot in the door while their parents pay their rent. And they often have access to parents' social networks to help them find jobs. Surveys revealed that working-class and first-generation college graduates, regardless of their career field, are more likely than their wealthier peers to end up in low-paying unskilled jobs when they pursue their passion.\nColleges, workplaces and career counselors who promote the \"follow your passion\" path for everyone, without leveling the playing field, help perpetuate socioeconomic inequalities among career aspirants.\nIt's not just well-off passion-seekers who benefit from the passion principle. Employers of passionate workers do, too. Potential employers showed greater interest in passionate applicants in part because they believed the applicants would work hard at their jobs without expecting an increase in pay. They even sacrifice a good salary, job stability and leisure time to work in a job they love.", "52": "After earning a bachelor's degree, I was determined to do what I love. I headed straight to graduate school to investigate the social problems that fascinated me.\nFor almost a decade, I told everyone I encountered that they should do the same. \"Follow your passion,\" I counseled. \"You can figure out the employment stuff later.\"\nIt wasn't until I began to research this widely accepted career advice that I understood how problematic it really was.\nAs a sociologist, I interviewed college students and professional workers to learn what it really meant to pursue their dreams, which I will refer to here as the passion principle. I was stunned by what I found out about this principle in the research for my new book.\nSurveys show the American public has long held the passion principle in high regard as a career decision-making priority. And its popularity is even stronger among those facing job instability.\nAdvocates of the passion principle found it compelling because they believed that following one's passion can provide workers with both the motivation necessary to work hard and a place to find fulfillment.\nYet, what I found is that following one's passion does not necessarily lead to fulfillment, but is one of the most powerful cultural forces perpetuating overwork. I also found that promoting the pursuit of one's passion helps perpetuate social inequalities due to the fact that not everyone has the same economic resources to allow them to pursue their passion with ease.\nWhile the passion principle is broadly popular, not everyone has the necessary resources to turn their passion into a stable, good-paying job. Passion-seekers from wealthy families are better able to wait until a job they are passionate about comes along without worrying about student loans in the meantime. They are also better situated to take unpaid internships to get their foot in the door while their parents pay their rent. And they often have access to parents' social networks to help them find jobs. Surveys revealed that working-class and first-generation college graduates, regardless of their career field, are more likely than their wealthier peers to end up in low-paying unskilled jobs when they pursue their passion.\nColleges, workplaces and career counselors who promote the \"follow your passion\" path for everyone, without leveling the playing field, help perpetuate socioeconomic inequalities among career aspirants.\nIt's not just well-off passion-seekers who benefit from the passion principle. Employers of passionate workers do, too. Potential employers showed greater interest in passionate applicants in part because they believed the applicants would work hard at their jobs without expecting an increase in pay. They even sacrifice a good salary, job stability and leisure time to work in a job they love.", "53": "After earning a bachelor's degree, I was determined to do what I love. I headed straight to graduate school to investigate the social problems that fascinated me.\nFor almost a decade, I told everyone I encountered that they should do the same. \"Follow your passion,\" I counseled. \"You can figure out the employment stuff later.\"\nIt wasn't until I began to research this widely accepted career advice that I understood how problematic it really was.\nAs a sociologist, I interviewed college students and professional workers to learn what it really meant to pursue their dreams, which I will refer to here as the passion principle. I was stunned by what I found out about this principle in the research for my new book.\nSurveys show the American public has long held the passion principle in high regard as a career decision-making priority. And its popularity is even stronger among those facing job instability.\nAdvocates of the passion principle found it compelling because they believed that following one's passion can provide workers with both the motivation necessary to work hard and a place to find fulfillment.\nYet, what I found is that following one's passion does not necessarily lead to fulfillment, but is one of the most powerful cultural forces perpetuating overwork. I also found that promoting the pursuit of one's passion helps perpetuate social inequalities due to the fact that not everyone has the same economic resources to allow them to pursue their passion with ease.\nWhile the passion principle is broadly popular, not everyone has the necessary resources to turn their passion into a stable, good-paying job. Passion-seekers from wealthy families are better able to wait until a job they are passionate about comes along without worrying about student loans in the meantime. They are also better situated to take unpaid internships to get their foot in the door while their parents pay their rent. And they often have access to parents' social networks to help them find jobs. Surveys revealed that working-class and first-generation college graduates, regardless of their career field, are more likely than their wealthier peers to end up in low-paying unskilled jobs when they pursue their passion.\nColleges, workplaces and career counselors who promote the \"follow your passion\" path for everyone, without leveling the playing field, help perpetuate socioeconomic inequalities among career aspirants.\nIt's not just well-off passion-seekers who benefit from the passion principle. Employers of passionate workers do, too. Potential employers showed greater interest in passionate applicants in part because they believed the applicants would work hard at their jobs without expecting an increase in pay. They even sacrifice a good salary, job stability and leisure time to work in a job they love.", "54": "After earning a bachelor's degree, I was determined to do what I love. I headed straight to graduate school to investigate the social problems that fascinated me.\nFor almost a decade, I told everyone I encountered that they should do the same. \"Follow your passion,\" I counseled. \"You can figure out the employment stuff later.\"\nIt wasn't until I began to research this widely accepted career advice that I understood how problematic it really was.\nAs a sociologist, I interviewed college students and professional workers to learn what it really meant to pursue their dreams, which I will refer to here as the passion principle. I was stunned by what I found out about this principle in the research for my new book.\nSurveys show the American public has long held the passion principle in high regard as a career decision-making priority. And its popularity is even stronger among those facing job instability.\nAdvocates of the passion principle found it compelling because they believed that following one's passion can provide workers with both the motivation necessary to work hard and a place to find fulfillment.\nYet, what I found is that following one's passion does not necessarily lead to fulfillment, but is one of the most powerful cultural forces perpetuating overwork. I also found that promoting the pursuit of one's passion helps perpetuate social inequalities due to the fact that not everyone has the same economic resources to allow them to pursue their passion with ease.\nWhile the passion principle is broadly popular, not everyone has the necessary resources to turn their passion into a stable, good-paying job. Passion-seekers from wealthy families are better able to wait until a job they are passionate about comes along without worrying about student loans in the meantime. They are also better situated to take unpaid internships to get their foot in the door while their parents pay their rent. And they often have access to parents' social networks to help them find jobs. Surveys revealed that working-class and first-generation college graduates, regardless of their career field, are more likely than their wealthier peers to end up in low-paying unskilled jobs when they pursue their passion.\nColleges, workplaces and career counselors who promote the \"follow your passion\" path for everyone, without leveling the playing field, help perpetuate socioeconomic inequalities among career aspirants.\nIt's not just well-off passion-seekers who benefit from the passion principle. Employers of passionate workers do, too. Potential employers showed greater interest in passionate applicants in part because they believed the applicants would work hard at their jobs without expecting an increase in pay. They even sacrifice a good salary, job stability and leisure time to work in a job they love.", "55": "After earning a bachelor's degree, I was determined to do what I love. I headed straight to graduate school to investigate the social problems that fascinated me.\nFor almost a decade, I told everyone I encountered that they should do the same. \"Follow your passion,\" I counseled. \"You can figure out the employment stuff later.\"\nIt wasn't until I began to research this widely accepted career advice that I understood how problematic it really was.\nAs a sociologist, I interviewed college students and professional workers to learn what it really meant to pursue their dreams, which I will refer to here as the passion principle. I was stunned by what I found out about this principle in the research for my new book.\nSurveys show the American public has long held the passion principle in high regard as a career decision-making priority. And its popularity is even stronger among those facing job instability.\nAdvocates of the passion principle found it compelling because they believed that following one's passion can provide workers with both the motivation necessary to work hard and a place to find fulfillment.\nYet, what I found is that following one's passion does not necessarily lead to fulfillment, but is one of the most powerful cultural forces perpetuating overwork. I also found that promoting the pursuit of one's passion helps perpetuate social inequalities due to the fact that not everyone has the same economic resources to allow them to pursue their passion with ease.\nWhile the passion principle is broadly popular, not everyone has the necessary resources to turn their passion into a stable, good-paying job. Passion-seekers from wealthy families are better able to wait until a job they are passionate about comes along without worrying about student loans in the meantime. They are also better situated to take unpaid internships to get their foot in the door while their parents pay their rent. And they often have access to parents' social networks to help them find jobs. Surveys revealed that working-class and first-generation college graduates, regardless of their career field, are more likely than their wealthier peers to end up in low-paying unskilled jobs when they pursue their passion.\nColleges, workplaces and career counselors who promote the \"follow your passion\" path for everyone, without leveling the playing field, help perpetuate socioeconomic inequalities among career aspirants.\nIt's not just well-off passion-seekers who benefit from the passion principle. Employers of passionate workers do, too. Potential employers showed greater interest in passionate applicants in part because they believed the applicants would work hard at their jobs without expecting an increase in pay. They even sacrifice a good salary, job stability and leisure time to work in a job they love."}, "questions": [{"id": "2025-06-1-l-1", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 1, "stem": "What did the woman do last Tuesday?", "options": ["A) Met the computer technician.", "B) Told the man about her trouble.", "C) Called the man's company.", "D) Visited Alpha Maintenance."], "answer": "C", "analysis": "【答案】C （细节题） Called the man's company.\n【定位】I spoke to a lady there last Tuesday, which is now 10 days ago. I made it clear it was an\nurgent matter and please send someone as soon as possible.\n我上周二跟你们那边一位女士通过话，到现在已经十天了。我讲得很清楚这是急事，请尽快\n派人来。\n【信号】时间锚 last Tuesday 后的应答。女士主动说 I spoke to a lady there last Tuesday，这里\n的 last Tuesday 正对上题干的时间锚，紧跟的动作 spoke to a lady there（there 指上文\n男士提到的 central office）就是她那天做的事，这就是答案句。\n【替换】I spoke to a lady there last Tuesday ↔ Called the man's company\n通话沟通改写成打电话给公司\n【排除】A) 原词陷阱\n选项盗用了对话开头出现的 computer technician 一词，但她见到这位技术员是今天上门\n这一刻，并非上周二；上周二她做的是打电话，时间对不上，属答非所问。\n借⽤原⽂：I'm the computer technician\nB) 张冠李戴\n上周二她是给公司打电话，电话那头是 a lady，而男士本人这周才接到任务上门，上周二两\n人根本没接触，把通话对象说成 the man 是张冠李戴。\n借⽤原⽂：I spoke to a lady there last Tuesday\nD) 原词陷阱\n选项盗用男士自报的 Alpha Maintenance，但那是他工作的公司名，女士只是打电话过\n去，并没有亲自上门拜访，visited 这个动作无中生有。\n借⽤原⽂：I work for Alpha Maintenance", "points": ["听力·长对话", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2025-06-1-l-2", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 2, "stem": "What does the man say he did when he got the call?", "options": ["A) Consulted someone in charge.", "B) Came as soon as possible.", "C) Informed the central office at once.", "D) Designated an engineer to the case."], "answer": "B", "analysis": "【答案】B （细节题） Came as soon as possible.\n【定位】I'm sorry, but I'm not in charge of scheduling all logistics. I simply do not know. All I can tell\nyou is I got the call this week and came as soon as I could.\n抱歉，调度安排不归我管，我确实不清楚。我能告诉你的就是，我这周接到电话，已经尽快\n赶过来了。\n【信号】被问者的直接应答句 All I can tell you is。女士追问他能不能早点来，男士的应答里 All I\ncan tell you is I got the call this week and came as soon as I could 用 All I can tell\nyou is 把重点框出来，这句他亲口交代自己接电话后的动作，是答案句。\n【替换】came as soon as I could ↔ Came as soon as possible\n尽我所能快换成尽可能快\n【排除】A) 与原文相反\n选项的 in charge 直接盗用原文 I'm not in charge of scheduling all logistics，但原文是\n否定，他强调调度不归他负责、自己也不清楚，根本没说去请示负责人，正好与原文相反。\n借⽤原⽂：I'm not in charge of scheduling all logistics\nC) 张冠李戴\ncentral office 来自男士早先的推测 someone from your office called our central\noffice，那是女方办公室的人打给总部，不是他接电话后做的；把别人的动作安到他头上，\n是张冠李戴。\n借⽤原⽂：someone from your office called our central office\nD) 原词陷阱\ndesignated an engineer 盗用 I'm the engineer designated to your case，那是他猜测\n自己被指派的过程、动作发出方是公司，不是他本人接电话后所为，属答非所问。\n借⽤原⽂：I'm the engineer designated to your case", "points": ["听力·长对话", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2025-06-1-l-3", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 3, "stem": "What is the feeling about the problem around the woman's workplace?", "options": ["A) Frustration.", "B) Intimidation.", "C) Desperation.", "D) Indignation."], "answer": "D", "analysis": "【答案】D （态度观点题） Indignation.\n【定位】Well, I'll have you know, my manager is rather indignant that it's taken you this long to come.\n这么说吧，我得让你知道，我的经理对你们拖这么久才来相当愤慨。\n【信号】评价性形容词 indignant。女士说 my manager is rather indignant that it's taken you\nthis long to come，indignant 是直接的情绪形容词，又被 rather 加重，正是描述工作场\n所对这件事感受的答案句。\n【替换】my manager is rather indignant ↔ Indignation\n形容词 indignant 转成名词 Indignation\n【排除】A) 未提及\nFrustration 是常见情绪词的合理猜测，但原文用的明确是 indignant（愤慨），二者程度\n和指向不同：indignant 是对怠慢的气愤，不是单纯的受挫，原文并未表达 frustration。\nB) 未提及\nIntimidation 指被威胁、被吓住，对话里女方是在抱怨对方来得慢，没有任何被恐吓的内\n容，与原文情绪不符，属未提及。\nC) 过度推断\nDesperation 表示绝望、走投无路，程度远超原文。女方只是不满拖延、表达气愤，并未到\n绝望地步，属过度推断。", "points": ["听力·长对话", "态度观点题"]}, {"id": "2025-06-1-l-4", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 4, "stem": "What does the woman say her manager would know?", "options": ["A) Whether the contents have been backed up.", "B) Whether they can find help somewhere else.", "C) Whether all data stored on the hard drive has got lost.", "D) Whether they need to wipe the system directories clean."], "answer": "A", "analysis": "【答案】A （细节题） Whether the contents have been backed up.\n【定位】M: Do you know if it's backed up? ... W: My manager would know. Let me go get him.\n男：你知道它有没有做备份吗？……女：我的经理会知道，我去把他叫来。\n【信号】一问一答的指代承接。男士先问 Do you know if it's backed up?，他随后解释 it 指 its\ncontents have been copied and saved somewhere else；女士最后说 My manager\nwould know，这句 would know 承接的正是前面备份这个问题，所以经理会知道的就是有\n没有备份。\n【替换】Do you know if it's backed up? ↔ Whether the contents have been backed up\nif it's backed up 改写成 whether…backed up\n【排除】B) 未提及\n对话里没有任何关于到别处找帮助的内容，女士只说叫经理来确认备份情况，find help\nsomewhere else 是无中生有，属未提及。\nC) 原词陷阱\n选项盗用男士那句 all data that was stored on its hard drive will be forever lost，但那\n是他对不备份后果的假设性说明，并不是女士说经理会知道的内容；经理会知道的是有没有\n备份，不是数据是否已全丢，属答非所问。\n借⽤原⽂：all data that was stored on its hard drive will be forever lost\nD) 原词陷阱\nwipe the system directories clean 直接取自 if I need to wipe the system directories\nclean，那是男士对自己后续可能操作的描述，是他要不要做的事，不是女士说经理会知道\n的内容，属答非所问。\n借⽤原⽂：if I need to wipe the system directories clean in order to reconfigure default protocols", "points": ["听力·长对话", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2025-06-1-l-5", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 5, "stem": "What will many people say about a career in law?", "options": ["A) It's boring.", "B) It's challenging.", "C) It's a beautiful thing.", "D) It's unlike most jobs."], "answer": "A", "analysis": "【答案】A （态度观点题） It's boring.\n【定位】A career in law is a beautiful thing in my opinion. Many people will say it's a boring field, but\nI disagree.\n在我看来，从事法律是件美好的事。许多人会说这是个枯燥的领域，但我不同意。\n【信号】归属信号 Many people will say。答案落点不在 a beautiful thing in my opinion，而在\nMany people will say it's a boring field 这句——题干问的是 many people，必须抓住\nMany people will say 这个明确的归属信号，它后面跟的才是『许多人』的说法。\n【替换】a boring field ↔ It's boring.\n同义浓缩\n【排除】C) 张冠李戴\nIt's a beautiful thing. 盗用了录音原话 a beautiful thing in my opinion，但那是男士『我\n个人』的看法，题干问的是 many people 怎么说——把说话人的观点安到『许多人』头\n上，归属错位。\n借⽤原⽂：a beautiful thing in my opinion\nB) 未提及\nIt's challenging. 听上去合理，但录音里无论是 many people 还是说话人，都没说过从事\n法律『有挑战性』，属凭常识脑补、原文无据。\nD) 未提及\nIt's unlike most jobs. 在录音中找不到对应，男士只说了 many people 觉得 boring、自己\n觉得 beautiful，从未拿它和『多数工作』作比较。", "points": ["听力·长对话", "态度观点题"]}, {"id": "2025-06-1-l-6", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 6, "stem": "What does the man say most of his work involves?", "options": ["A) Arbitrating between disagreeing solicitors.", "B) Preventing disputes from escalating.", "C) Buying and selling property.", "D) Mediating land disputes."], "answer": "D", "analysis": "【答案】D （细节题） Mediating land disputes.\n【定位】I'm a solicitor, specializing in real estate. Most of my work involves mediating land disputes.\n我是一名事务律师，专攻房地产。我的工作大部分涉及调解土地纠纷。\n【信号】限定信号 Most of my work。录音 Most of my work involves mediating land disputes\n与题干的 most of his work 用词几乎一致，Most of my work 就是定位信号，它后面跟的\n动作即答案。\n【替换】mediating land disputes ↔ Mediating land disputes.\n近乎原词对应\n【排除】C) 原词陷阱\nBuying and selling property. 蹭了录音 specializing in real estate 的房地产话题，听到\n『房产』就想选；但男士说的主要工作是 mediating land disputes，不是买卖房产，属答\n非所问的原词陷阱。\n借⽤原⽂：specializing in real estate\nA) 偷换概念\nArbitrating between disagreeing solicitors. 借用了后文 arbitrate between two\nclashing parties 的 arbitrate，但把对象偷换成 disagreeing solicitors(意见不合的律师)；\n录音里仲裁的对象是两个地主/当事人，不是律师，对象错位。\n借⽤原⽂：arbitrate between two clashing parties\nB) 答非所问\nPreventing disputes from escalating. 对应后文 prevents a problem from escalating，\n但那是女士事后归纳男士工作意义的话，且题干问的是 most of his work 的具体内容；用\n『防止问题升级』这个意义层面的概括来答『主要做什么』，答非所问。\n借⽤原⽂：prevents a problem from escalating", "points": ["听力·长对话", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2025-06-1-l-7", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 7, "stem": "Why does the man say it's usually better to resolve problems outside of court whenever possible?", "options": ["A) Courts are intended for fixing major legal disputes.", "B) Courts are getting too bureaucratic to function.", "C) Courts can be frustrating and expensive.", "D) Courts can be frightening and arbitrary."], "answer": "C", "analysis": "【答案】C （细节题） Courts can be frustrating and expensive.\n【定位】You need to remember that courts, like all bureaucracies really, can be very messy,\nfrustrating, and expensive. For this reason, it's usually better to resolve problems outside of\ncourt whenever possible.\n你要记住，法庭和所有官僚机构一样，可能非常混乱、令人沮丧而且昂贵。正因如此，能在\n庭外解决问题通常更好。\n【信号】因果信号 For this reason。For this reason 是明确的因果连接词，它前面那句\ncourts...can be very messy, frustrating, and expensive 就是『为什么庭外解决更好』的\n原因，顺着 For this reason 往前一句即定位到答案。\n【替换】frustrating, and expensive ↔ Courts can be frustrating and expensive.\n原词提取，去掉多余特征\n【排除】D) 偷换概念\nCourts can be frightening and arbitrary. 与正确项仅差两个形容词，但录音说的是\nfrustrating(沮丧)和 expensive(昂贵)，不是 frightening(可怕)和 arbitrary(武断)；这两个\n词录音根本没念，是替换掉真实形容词的近形陷阱。\nA) 答非所问\nCourts are intended for fixing major legal disputes. 说的是法庭的用途，录音里男士并\n没有用『法庭是用来解决重大纠纷的』来解释为何庭外更好，方向不符。\nB) 过度推断\nCourts are getting too bureaucratic to function. 蹭了录音 like all bureaucracies 的\nbureaucracy，但录音只是把法庭比作官僚机构、说它混乱昂贵，从没说法庭『过于官僚以\n致无法运转』，属由原词过度推断。\n借⽤原⽂：like all bureaucracies", "points": ["听力·长对话", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2025-06-1-l-8", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 8, "stem": "What does the man think is required of any law professional?", "options": ["A) The ability to make arguments in a unique way.", "B) The skill of preventing conflicts between parties concerned.", "C) The skill of foreseeing any potential stakes in their work.", "D) The ability to express themselves clearly and forcefully."], "answer": "D", "analysis": "【答案】D （细节题） The ability to express themselves clearly and forcefully.\n【定位】Any job that involves people requires good interpersonal skills, and any law professional\nneeds to be very eloquent at articulating that argument.\n任何与人打交道的工作都需要良好的人际交往能力，而任何法律从业者都需要非常善于清楚\n地表达论点。\n【信号】要求信号 needs to be。题干问 required of any law professional，录音 any law\nprofessional needs to be very eloquent at articulating that argument 用 needs to 直\n接回应，needs to be 就是要求信号，其后描述的能力即答案。\n【替换】eloquent at articulating that argument ↔\nThe ability to express themselves clearly and forcefully.\n同义替换\n【排除】B) 原词陷阱\nThe skill of preventing conflicts between parties concerned. 蹭了前文 prevents a\nproblem from escalating 与调解纠纷的整段话题，听上去和这职业最贴；但题干问 any\nlaw professional 被要求的能力，录音对此的回答是 eloquent at articulating，不是预防\n冲突，属答非所问的原词陷阱。\n借⽤原⽂：prevents a problem from escalating\nA) 偷换概念\nThe ability to make arguments in a unique way. 借了录音 articulating that argument\n的 argument，但录音只说要 eloquent(善于表达)，从未说要『以独特的方式』论辩，\nunique 是凭空加上的限定。\n借⽤原⽂：articulating that argument\nC) 张冠李戴\nThe skill of foreseeing any potential stakes in their work. 盗用了结尾 the stakes are\nhigher in criminal law 的 stakes，但那是男士比较两类法律时说的话，与『法律从业者被\n要求的能力』无关，且录音从没说要『预见潜在利害』。\n借⽤原⽂：the stakes are higher in criminal law", "points": ["听力·长对话", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2025-06-1-l-9", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 9, "stem": "What did the researchers do in their review in the journal Trends in Cognitive Sciences?", "options": ["A) They looked into the relationship between one's prior knowledge and creativity.", "B) They offered novel ways to help older adults to keep their memories from aging.", "C) They proposed an explanation for old people's difficulty in retrieving memories.", "D) They advanced a new model concerning human information storage and retrieval."], "answer": "C", "analysis": "【答案】C （细节题）\nThey proposed an explanation for old people's difficulty in retrieving memories.\n【定位】researchers propose an explanation for why this might be happening: the brains of older\nadults allocate more space to accumulated knowledge and have more material to navigate\nwhen attempting to access memories.\n研究人员提出了一个解释，说明为什么会发生这种情况：老年人的大脑把更多空间分配给积\n累的知识，因而在试图提取记忆时要导航的材料更多。\n【信号】研究行为动词 propose。定位信号是描述研究者行为的那个动词 propose an explanation\nfor why this might be happening——题干问 do（做了什么），原文里紧跟 researchers\n的那个动作动词 propose 就是答案句标志。\n【替换】propose an explanation for why this might be happening ↔ proposed an explanation\n近乎同词的动作\nwhy this might be happening ↔ old people's difficulty in retrieving memories\n指代还原为具体所指\n【排除】A) 原词陷阱\nA 项 looked into the relationship between one's prior knowledge and creativity 盗用\n了原文确实出现过的 prior knowledge 与 creativity（见第四段 enhanced, creativity），\n但那是后文讲记忆模式的好处时顺带提到的，研究综述本身做的核心动作是 propose an\nexplanation，不是去研究知识与创造力的关系，属听到原词就想选的陷阱。\n借⽤原⽂：older adults may be well served by their prior knowledge\nB) 未提及\nB 项 offered novel ways to help older adults to keep their memories from aging 借用\n了 older adults、memories 等话题词，但原文从头到尾只是提出解释、分析现象，没有任\n何研究者提供新方法去防止记忆衰老的内容，属未提及。\nD) 偷换概念\nD 项 advanced a new model concerning human information storage and retrieval 用\nretrieval 这个原文反复出现的词制造熟悉感，但原文研究者做的是 propose an\nexplanation（提出一个解释），不是 advance a new model（提出一个新模型）；一个解\n释和一个新模型不是同一层级的成果，属偷换概念。\n借⽤原⽂：propose an explanation for why this might be happening", "points": ["听力·篇章", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2025-06-1-l-10", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 10, "stem": "What do several behavioral and brain imaging studies show?", "options": ["A) Young adults rely on memory while older adults keep notes as a reminder.", "B) Older adults often retrieve irrelevant memories along with what they want.", "C) Young adults accumulate knowledge much more quickly than older adults.", "D) Older adults generally perform cognitive tasks much slower than young adults."], "answer": "B", "analysis": "【答案】B （细节题） Older adults often retrieve irrelevant memories along with what they want.\n【定位】studies, which show that older adults have difficulty suppressing information that is no longer\nrelevant and that when searching for a specific memory, they often retrieve other, irrelevant\nmemories along with it.\n这些研究显示，老年人难以抑制不再相关的信息，并且在搜寻某一具体记忆时，常常连带提\n取出其他无关的记忆。\n【信号】show that 引出研究结论。信号是 which show that——题干问 studies show，原文里\nstudies which show that 之后的内容就是答案句，紧跟其后列出的 retrieve other,\nirrelevant memories 正是落点。\n【替换】they often retrieve other, irrelevant memories along with it ↔\nOlder adults often retrieve irrelevant memories along with what they want\n代词还原后近乎同句\n【排除】A) 未提及\nA 项 Young adults rely on memory while older adults keep notes as a reminder 听着\n像在讲两类人记忆方式，但原文只说老年人更依赖旧知识，从没说老年人靠记笔记提醒、也\n没说年轻人靠记忆，属未提及。\nC) 原词陷阱\nC 项 Young adults accumulate knowledge much more quickly than older adults 盗用\n了原文的 accumulated knowledge（见第二段 accumulated knowledge），但原文讲的\n是老年人积累的知识多、要导航的材料多，从没比较谁积累得更快，选项凭空加了 much\nmore quickly 的速度比较，属过度推断兼偷换。\n借⽤原⽂：allocate more space to accumulated knowledge\nD) 原词陷阱\nD 项 Older adults generally perform cognitive tasks much slower than young adults\n盗用了原文的 cognitive task（见第三段 when given a cognitive task），但原文只说做\n认知任务时老年人更依赖旧知识，根本没提速度更慢，选项擅自加了 much slower 的速度\n论断，属原词陷阱加无中生有。\n借⽤原⽂：when given a cognitive task, older adults rely more heavily on previous knowledge", "points": ["听力·篇章", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2025-06-1-l-11", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 11, "stem": "What does evidence suggest regarding older adults?", "options": ["A) They show preserved, and sometimes enhanced, creativity.", "B) They frequently suffer from disorderly crowded memories.", "C) They can rely on their accumulated wisdom in an emergency.", "D) They may well be served by forgetting their prior knowledge."], "answer": "A", "analysis": "【答案】A （细节题） They show preserved, and sometimes enhanced, creativity.\n【定位】\"Evidence suggests that older adults show preserved, and at times enhanced, creativity as a\nfunction of enriched memories,\" the researchers write.\n研究人员写道：证据表明，由于记忆更丰富，老年人的创造力得以保留、有时甚至得到\n增强。\n【信号】Evidence suggests 引出证据结论。信号是 Evidence suggests that——题干用的就是\nevidence suggest，原文这处 Evidence suggests that 之后说的 older adults show\npreserved, and at times enhanced, creativity 即答案句。\n【替换】older adults show preserved, and at times enhanced, creativity ↔\nThey show preserved, and sometimes enhanced, creativity\n仅换一个程度词\n【排除】B) 原词陷阱\nB 项 They frequently suffer from disorderly crowded memories 盗用了原文的\ndisorderly, crowded memories（见第四段 disorderly, crowded memories），但原文那\n处是讲这些记忆模式带来的困难，而本题问的是 evidence suggests 那句的正面发现（创\n造力），方向与问点都不对，属原词陷阱兼答非所问。\n借⽤原⽂：these disorderly, crowded memories may pose\nC) 偷换概念\nC 项 They can rely on their accumulated wisdom in an emergency 盗用了原文的\naccumulated wisdom（见第四段 accumulated wisdom），但原文说的是在 decision-\nmaking（决策）时可借助积累的智慧，并非 in an emergency（紧急情况），选项把决策\n场景偷换成应急场景，属偷换概念。\n借⽤原⽂：may be well served by their prior knowledge when it comes to decision-making\nD) 与原文相反\nD 项 They may well be served by forgetting their prior knowledge 盗用了原文 may\nbe well served by 与 prior knowledge 的近原句（见第四段 may be well served by\ntheir prior knowledge），但原文说老年人是靠旧知识 served，选项却改成靠忘掉旧知识\nserved，把方向反了，属与原文相反。\n借⽤原⽂：older adults may be well served by their prior knowledge", "points": ["听力·篇章", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2025-06-1-l-12", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 12, "stem": "What do we learn about American workers from the passage?", "options": ["A) They are actually proud of the goods and services they produce.", "B) They are worried about being alienated from the outside world.", "C) They are rarely in control of many things related to their work.", "D) They are longing to share the profits made from their work."], "answer": "C", "analysis": "【答案】C （细节题） They are rarely in control of many things related to their work.\n【定位】Workers are often not in control of how they work, when they work, what is done with the\ngoods and services they produce, and what is done with the profits made from their work.\n工人们往往无法掌控自己如何工作、何时工作、他们生产的产品和服务被如何处置、以及他\n们劳动所创造的利润被如何使用。\n【信号】分点首句\"First\"后的解释句。本段以 First, work can be alienating. 起头点出第一个问\n题，紧接着的这句 Workers are often not in control of 就是对该问题的展开说明，是承载\n答案的句子。\n【替换】Workers are often not in control of ↔\nThey are rarely in control of many things related to their work\noften not 改写为 rarely\nhow they work, when they work, what is done with the goods and services they produce, and what is\ndone with the profits\n↔ many things related to their work\n罗列细节概括为 many things\n【排除】A) 原词陷阱\nA 借用录音原词 the goods and services they produce 制造熟悉感，但录音说的是工\n人\"无法掌控\"产品去向，A 却说工人\"为之自豪\"，态度与原文相反。\n借⽤原⽂：what is done with the goods and services they produce\nB) 原词陷阱\nB 盗用录音 work can be alienating 的 alienat- 词根，但录音的\"异化\"指工人与其劳动/产\n出相疏离，B 却说成\"担心被外部世界孤立\"，偷换了概念。\n借⽤原⽂：First, work can be alienating.\nD) 原词陷阱\nD 借用录音原词 the profits made from their work，但录音说的是工人\"无法掌控利润去\n向\"，D 却说工人\"渴望分享利润\"，是录音根本没提的引申。\n借⽤原⽂：what is done with the profits made from their work", "points": ["听力·篇章", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2025-06-1-l-13", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 13, "stem": "What does the passage say long working hours contribute to?", "options": ["A) The steady decrease in productivity.", "B) The adverse effect on physical health.", "C) The feeling of being time poor.", "D) The rising economic inequality."], "answer": "C", "analysis": "【答案】C （细节题） The feeling of being time poor.\n【定位】Long working hours contribute to the feeling of \"time poverty\", which has an adverse effect\non psychological well-being.\n长时间工作会带来\"时间贫乏\"的感受，这种感受又会对心理健康产生不良影响。\n【信号】因果信号 contribute to。句中 Long working hours contribute to the feeling of \"time\npoverty\" 与题干同词出现，contribute to 这个因果信号直接锁定\"长工时→时间贫乏\"，是\n答案句。\n【替换】the feeling of \"time poverty\" ↔ The feeling of being time poor.\ntime poverty 与 time poor 同根改写\n【排除】A) 与原文相反\nA 借录音里出现过的 productivity increases 中的 productivity，但录音说的是\"尽管生产\n率提高\"（上升），A 却说\"生产率持续下降\"，与原文方向相反。\n借⽤原⽂：full time employees work an average of 8.72 hours per day despite\nproductivity increases\nB) 偷换概念\nB 几乎照搬录音 has an adverse effect on psychological well-being 的 adverse effect，\n却把 psychological（心理）换成 physical（身体）健康，偷改了范围。\n借⽤原⽂：which has an adverse effect on psychological well-being\nD) 张冠李戴\nD 的 economic inequality 出自第3段 driving economic inequality，那是\"工资跟不上生\n产率\"的后果，并非长工时的后果，被错搬到本题。\n借⽤原⽂：Real wages have not kept pace with productivity, driving economic inequality", "points": ["听力·篇章", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2025-06-1-l-14", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 14, "stem": "What does automation do, according to the passage?", "options": ["A) It alters the structure of work.", "B) It puts jobs and wages at risk.", "C) It liberates people from tedious and laborious work.", "D) It creates new work opportunities in the IT industry."], "answer": "B", "analysis": "【答案】B （细节题） It puts jobs and wages at risk.\n【定位】Fourth, automation puts jobs and wages at risk.\n第四，自动化使工作岗位和工资面临风险。\n【信号】分点首句直接定调。本段分点句 Fourth, automation puts jobs and wages at risk. 开门\n见山给 automation 定调，是直接承载答案的主题句。\n【替换】automation puts jobs and wages at risk ↔ It puts jobs and wages at risk.\n近乎原句对接\n【排除】A) 张冠李戴\nA 的 alter the structure of work 借用录音 unless changes are made to the structure of\nwork，但那是\"除非改变工作结构\"的假设条件，并非 automation 本身所做的事。\n借⽤原⽂：unless changes are made to the structure of work\nC) 原词陷阱\nC 借录音 liberate people from the 40-hour work week 的 liberate，但原文是\nTechnological innovation could in theory liberate——只是\"理论上可能\"，并被随后的\nBut 推翻，并非自动化实际的作为。\n借⽤原⽂：Technological innovation could in theory liberate people from the 40-hour work week.\nBut unless changes are made\nD) 未提及\n录音只说 automation 会 further diminish work opportunities（进一步减少工作机\n会），从未说它在IT业创造新岗位，D 属无中生有。", "points": ["听力·篇章", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2025-06-1-l-15", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 15, "stem": "What do post-work theorists advocate?", "options": ["A) Finding meaning in work.", "B) Prioritizing life over work.", "C) Improving relationships in the community.", "D) Realizing one's social value in the workplace."], "answer": "B", "analysis": "【答案】B （态度观点题） Prioritizing life over work.\n【定位】The post-work theorists believe that work should not hold a central position in our lives or\ntake up so much of our time. ... Thus, life should be seen as an end in itself.\n后工作理论家认为，工作不应在我们的生活中占据中心位置，也不应占用我们这么多时\n间。……因此，生活本身应被视为目的。\n【信号】表态动词 believe 与结论 Thus。The post-work theorists believe that work should not\nhold a central position in our lives 中的 believe 直接引出主张，段末 Thus, life should\nbe seen as an end in itself. 以 Thus 收束出结论，两处合起来锁定立场。\n【替换】work should not hold a central position in our lives or take up so much of our time ↔\nPrioritizing life over work.\n工作不居中心等于生活优先\nlife should be seen as an end in itself ↔ Prioritizing life over work.\n生活本身是目的印证生活优先\n【排除】A) 与原文相反\nA 借录音 meaning, purpose and social value reside in the communities and\nrelationships built and sustained outside of the workplace 里的 meaning，但原文明\n说意义在职场\"之外\"，A 却说\"在工作中找意义\"，正话反说。\n借⽤原⽂：meaning, purpose and social value reside in the communities and relationships built and\nsustained outside of the workplace\nC) 原词陷阱\nC 借录音 the communities and relationships 里的 communities/relationships，但原文\n是说意义\"寓于\"社区与关系之中，并非主张\"去改善社区关系\"，把举例性说明当成了主张。\n借⽤原⽂：meaning, purpose and social value reside in the communities and relationships\nD) 与原文相反\nD 借录音 social value 一词，但原文说社会价值在职场\"之外\"的社区与关系里实现，D 却\n说\"在职场中实现社会价值\"，与原文相反。\n借⽤原⽂：social value reside in the communities and relationships built and sustained outside of\nthe workplace", "points": ["听力·篇章", "态度观点题"]}, {"id": "2025-06-1-l-16", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 16, "stem": "What does the speaker say even our personal beliefs about effort can do?", "options": ["A) Affect our attitude to novel tasks.", "B) Distinguish us in the social world.", "C) Outweigh IQ in importance.", "D) Impact academic achievements."], "answer": "D", "analysis": "【答案】D （细节题） Impact academic achievements.\n【定位】Even our personal beliefs about efforts can affect academic outcomes.\n甚至连我们对努力的个人信念，都能影响学业成果。\n【信号】题干原词复现 + Even 强调。录音 Even our personal beliefs about efforts can affect\nacademic outcomes. 几乎照搬了题干的 personal beliefs about effort，又用 Even 加重\n语气提示\"这点尤其值得注意\"——题干关键词原样出现加上 Even 的强调，就是答案句的\n信号。\n【替换】affect academic outcomes ↔ Impact academic achievements.\n近义词整体替换\n【排除】A) 张冠李戴\nAffect our attitude to novel tasks. 盗用了录音后文 a novel music toy / a novel task 里\n的 novel（新的）一词，但那是讲婴儿对\"新玩具 / 新任务\"，和本题\"个人信念能做什么\"无\n关，属张冠李戴。\n借⽤原⽂：a novel music toy\nB) 原词陷阱\nDistinguish us in the social world. 借了录音 keen observers of the social world 里的\nsocial world，但原文是说\"婴儿是社会世界的敏锐观察者\"，根本没说个人信念能\"在社会中\n区分我们\"，是盗用原词的答非所问。\n借⽤原⽂：keen observers of the social world\nC) 偷换概念\nOutweigh IQ in importance. 抓住了录音里的 IQ，但原文说自控力与坚持是\nindependence of IQ（独立于 IQ、与 IQ 无关），并没有说\"比 IQ 更重要\"，把\"无关\"偷换\n成了\"更重要\"，是与原文不符的偷换。\n借⽤原⽂：independence of IQ", "points": ["听力·讲座", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2025-06-1-l-17", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 17, "stem": "How did the researchers turn on the music toy in their experiment?", "options": ["A) By pressing a hidden button.", "B) By pushing a big button on top.", "C) By pressing two buttons at the same time.", "D) By helping the babies push the right button."], "answer": "A", "analysis": "【答案】A （细节题） By pressing a hidden button.\n【定位】Out of sight of the babies, we turned on the music toy with a hidden button so that they heard\nthe toy making music.\n在婴儿看不到的地方，我们用一个隐藏的按钮打开了音乐玩具，好让他们听到玩具发出\n音乐。\n【信号】题干动词 turned on 原样复现。录音 we turned on the music toy with a hidden\nbutton 把题干的 turn on the music toy 原样念出，紧跟 with a hidden button 交代方式\n——题干动作短语原样出现的那句，就是答案句。\n【替换】turned on the music toy with a hidden button ↔ By pressing a hidden button.\nwith a hidden button 概括为 by pressing it\n【排除】B) 原词陷阱\nBy pushing a big button on top. 整句盗自录音 pushing a big button on top，但原文\n说这个顶上的大按钮\"按得下去却其实不启动任何东西\"（didn't actually activate\nanything），它是给婴儿试的假按钮，不是研究者打开玩具的方式，是典型原词陷阱。\n借⽤原⽂：a big button on top, but didn't actually activate anything\nC) 未提及\nBy pressing two buttons at the same time. 里\"同时按两个按钮\"在录音中完全没有出现\n——原文只有一个隐藏按钮和一个假的大按钮，没有\"两个一起按\"的说法，属未提及。\nD) 与原文相反\nBy helping the babies push the right button. 与录音相反：原文是研究者 Out of sight\nof the babies（背着婴儿）自己用隐藏按钮打开，并非\"帮婴儿按对按钮\"，把\"偷偷开\"歪曲\n成\"协助婴儿按\"，与原文不符。\n借⽤原⽂：Out of sight of the babies, we turned on the music toy with a hidden button", "points": ["听力·讲座", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2025-06-1-l-18", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 18, "stem": "What could the infants do in the experiment according to the speaker?", "options": ["A) Perform difficult tasks successfully just by observing how adults acted.", "B) Make generalizable inferences about persistence from a few examples.", "C) Adapt themselves to different social contexts.", "D) Work hard to interact with experimenters."], "answer": "B", "analysis": "【答案】B （细节题） Make generalizable inferences about persistence from a few examples.\n【定位】They can generalize such abstract concepts as causal relationships and social roles from just a\nfew examples. Could infants also make broad generalizable inferences from a few examples\nwhen it comes to effort?\n他们能仅凭少数几个例子，就归纳出因果关系、社会角色这类抽象概念。那么，当涉及努力\n时，婴儿是否也能从少数例子中做出宽泛而可推广的推断呢？\n【信号】选项长句原话复现 generalizable inferences from a few examples。录音 make broad\ngeneralizable inferences from a few examples 几乎照搬了选项 B 的措辞，from just a\nfew examples 又反复强调\"只凭少数例子\"——选项学术长句在录音原话里成段复现，就是\n答案信号；第4段 they generalized the value of effort 进一步证实婴儿确实做到了这种\n推广。\n【替换】make broad generalizable inferences from a few examples ↔\nMake generalizable inferences about persistence from a few examples.\n近乎原词复现 + persistence 同义补足\n【排除】A) 过度推断\nPerform difficult tasks successfully just by observing how adults acted. 借了录音\nkeen observers / observing 的\"观察\"之意，但原文说的是婴儿从观察中\"做出推断、推广\n努力的价值\"，并没说他们\"光靠看就成功完成难任务\"；successfully、just by 把原意夸大\n了一截，是过度推断。\n借⽤原⽂：infants are keen observers of the social world\nC) 偷换概念\nAdapt themselves to different social contexts. 盗用录音 based on social context 的\nsocial context，但原文是说\"坚持也许是灵活的、随社会情境而变\"（指坚持这一特质的性\n质），不是说\"婴儿能让自己适应不同社会情境\"，主体和意思都被偷换。\n借⽤原⽂：it's flexible and adaptable based on social context\nD) 张冠李戴\nWork hard to interact with experimenters. 借了录音里反复出现的 experimenter（实验\n者）和 effort / work hard 之意，但原文说\"努力工作\"的是录像里的成人 an adult\nworking hard，婴儿是观察者，录音从没说婴儿\"努力去和实验者互动\"，是张冠李戴。\n借⽤原⽂：an adult working hard", "points": ["听力·讲座", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2025-06-1-l-19", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 19, "stem": "What is the speaker's purpose of giving this talk?", "options": ["A) Offering advice on overcoming habitual lateness.", "B) Exemplifying various recreational opportunities.", "C) Scrutinizing individuals' defining traits.", "D) Suggesting ways for setting priorities."], "answer": "A", "analysis": "【答案】A （目的题） Offering advice on overcoming habitual lateness.\n【定位】If chronic lateness has taken over your life and turned into a defining trait of who you are, it's\nlikely that you're losing out on job offers, recreational opportunities, friendships, and more. If\nyou are showing up late to your own life, get organized, prioritize punctuality, and address\nany underlying causes.\n如果长期迟到已经占据了你的生活、变成你这个人的一个鲜明特征,那你很可能正在失去工作\n机会、休闲机会、友谊等等。如果你连自己的人生都总是迟到,那就把自己安排好、把准时放\n在首位、并解决背后的根本原因。\n【信号】篇首点题句。目的题的答案信号是\"开头的总起句\":首段先点出\"chronic lateness\"(长期迟到)\n的危害,再用祈使句 get organized, prioritize punctuality, and address any underlying\ncauses 给出全篇要教的方向,后面各段(设两个闹钟、前一晚准备、中间留时间)都是这句的\n展开,故它统领全篇、就是目的所在。\n【替换】chronic lateness ↔ habitual lateness\n同义词替换\nget organized, prioritize punctuality, and address any underlying causes ↔\nOffering advice on overcoming\n详述概括为\"给建议\"\n【排除】B) 原词陷阱\nExemplifying various recreational opportunities. 盗用了首段的原词 recreational:录音里\nrecreational opportunities 只是迟到会\"失去\"的东西之一,被一笔带过,讲话根本不是在\"举\n例各种休闲机会\",属于把局部细节当主旨。\n借⽤原⽂：recreational opportunities\nC) 原词陷阱\nScrutinizing individuals' defining traits. 盗用了首段的 a defining trait of who you are:\n录音只是说\"长期迟到会变成你的一个鲜明特征\",并非要去\"剖析人的个性特质\",方向完全跑\n偏。\n借⽤原⽂：a defining trait of who you are\nD) 偷换概念\nSuggesting ways for setting priorities. 借了 prioritize punctuality(把准时放首位)里\n的\"priorit-\"做文章:录音只把\"优先准时\"作为众多建议中的一句,全篇并非教\"如何排优先级\",\n以偏概全。\n借⽤原⽂：prioritize punctuality", "points": ["听力·讲座", "目的题"]}, {"id": "2025-06-1-l-20", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 20, "stem": "What should you do if mornings are a struggle for you?", "options": ["A) Make the breakfast simpler.", "B) Take the alarms seriously.", "C) Ready yourself in the early dawn.", "D) Get prepared the night before."], "answer": "D", "analysis": "【答案】D （细节题） Get prepared the night before.\n【定位】If mornings are a struggle for you, do as much as you can to ready yourself the night before.\nBefore you go to bed, lay out your clothes and pack your bag for the next day.\n如果早晨对你来说很吃力,那就尽量在前一晚把自己准备好。睡觉前,把衣服摆好、把第二天\n的包收拾好。\n【信号】条件句原话复现+其后应对。答案信号是题干条件句在录音里的几乎原话复现:录音 If\nmornings are a struggle for you 与题干一字不差,紧跟其后的 do as much as you can to\nready yourself the night before 就是给出的做法,据\"条件句之后即答案\"锁定此句。\n【替换】ready yourself the night before ↔ Get prepared the night before.\n近义短语替换\n【排除】A) 未提及\nMake the breakfast simpler. 听着像应对\"早晨吃力\"的合理办法,但录音讲早晨那段只说\"前\n一晚摆衣服、收包\",从未提及早餐或把它简化,属凭常识脑补、原文未提。\nB) 张冠李戴\nTake the alarms seriously. 盗用了第三段的 take the alarms seriously:那是\"设两个闹\n钟\"那条建议里的话,和本题\"早晨吃力怎么办\"不是同一条,被错接到这道题上。\n借⽤原⽂：take the alarms seriously\nC) 与原文相反\nReady yourself in the early dawn. 把 ready yourself 的时间偷改成\"清晨(early dawn)\",\n而录音明说是 ready yourself the night before(前一晚),时间点正好相反,正中第一步预读标\n出的 C/D 对立陷阱。\n借⽤原⽂：ready yourself the night before", "points": ["听力·讲座", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2025-06-1-l-21", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 21, "stem": "According to the speaker, what can we do to combat unexpected delays?", "options": ["A) Finish the prior task 30 minutes earlier.", "B) Keep ourselves from hitting a bump.", "C) Leave time in between activities.", "D) Try to avoid possible hold-ups."], "answer": "C", "analysis": "【答案】C （细节题） Leave time in between activities.\n【定位】Learn to leave time in between tasks and meetings. As well as giving you space, this time also\nacts as a buffer between events, which can be borrowed from, should you be held up in a\nprior meeting, allowing you to still get to the next one on time.\n学会在各项任务和会议之间留出时间。这段时间除了给你余地,还在各项活动之间起到缓冲作\n用——万一前一场会议把你拖住了,可以从这段时间里挪用,让你仍能准时赶到下一场。\n【信号】段首主张句+buffer 缓冲解释。答案信号是第五段段首的核心主张 Learn to leave time in\nbetween tasks and meetings,其后用 acts as a buffer(充当缓冲)解释它正好能对付\"被前\n一场拖住\"的意外延误,与题干 combat unexpected delays 对上,据此锁定段首这句主张。\n【替换】leave time in between tasks and meetings ↔ Leave time in between activities.\n上位词概括替换\nacts as a buffer between events ↔ Leave time in between activities.\n缓冲机制印证该做法\n【排除】A) 原词陷阱\nFinish the prior task 30 minutes earlier. 盗用了末句的数字 add 10 to 30 minutes for\nunexpected delays 里的\"30 minutes\":录音是说\"为意外延误额外加留 10 到 30 分钟\",并不\n是\"把前一项任务提前 30 分钟完成\",数字念到了、事却拼错了。\n借⽤原⽂：add 10 to 30 minutes for unexpected delays\nB) 与原文相反\nKeep ourselves from hitting a bump. 盗用了 you'll arrive late as soon as you hit a\nbump 里的\"hit a bump\":录音是说\"一旦把日程排太满,碰到颠簸就会迟到\",讲的是过度排期\n的后果,而非教你\"避免碰到颠簸\";颠簸是无法避免的,要做的是留缓冲,选项把原意拧反了。\n借⽤原⽂：you'll arrive late as soon as you hit a bump\nD) 偷换概念\nTry to avoid possible hold-ups. 只是把题干目标\"应对意外延误\"换个说法重述一遍(avoid\nhold-ups),并未给出录音真正提出的可操作做法;且录音 should you be held up 把\"被拖\n住\"当成会发生、需用缓冲化解的事,而非教你\"避免\"它,这是空泛复述目标的陷阱。\n借⽤原⽂：should you be held up", "points": ["听力·讲座", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2025-06-1-l-22", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 22, "stem": "What do visitors find about the cities they've been to?", "options": ["A) Their lifestyles vary.", "B) Their traits vary.", "C) They have different customs.", "D) They have different feels."], "answer": "D", "analysis": "【答案】D （细节题） They have different feels.\n【定位】Different cities have different feels for visitors.\n不同的城市带给游客不同的感受。\n【信号】开篇点题句。这是第一段抛出两个问句后给出的第一句陈述 Different cities have different\nfeels for visitors.，独白常在开头先点主题，它直接对应题干问的\"游客的发现\"，是答\n案句。\n【替换】different feels for visitors ↔ They have different feels.\n近乎原句保留 feels\n【排除】A) 偷换概念\nTheir lifestyles vary. 把\"feels\"偷换成 lifestyles（生活方式），原文谈的是\"感受\"不是\"生\n活方式\"，属偷换概念。\nB) 偷换概念\nTheir traits vary. 用 traits（特征）顶替原文的 feels，原文从未说城市\"特征\"不同，是换词\n制造的干扰。\nC) 未提及\nThey have different customs. 的 customs（习俗）原文没有出现，属无中生有的替换项。", "points": ["听力·讲座", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2025-06-1-l-23", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 23, "stem": "What did one study find about Bostonians compared with residents of other U.S. cities?", "options": ["A) They are not as willing to help strangers.", "B) They are not as patient with one another.", "C) They violate traffic rules more frequently.", "D) They become more easily irritated in public."], "answer": "A", "analysis": "【答案】A （细节题） They are not as willing to help strangers.\n【定位】One study found that people here are less willing to volunteer to help strangers than those\nresidents of other U.S. cities.\n一项研究发现，与美国其它城市的居民相比，这里的人更不愿意主动帮助陌生人。\n【信号】study found 信号词。句首 One study found that 是题干 one study 的直接对应锚点，\n其后跟的就是研究结论，是答案句。\n【替换】less willing to volunteer to help strangers ↔ They are not as willing to help strangers.\nless=not as 同义改写\n【排除】B) 答非所问\nnot as patient with one another 里的 patient 来自后文 too busy to be polite 的语境联\n想，但研究句说的是\"帮陌生人\"不是\"彼此有无耐心\"，把别处内容安到研究结论上，属答非\n所问。\n借⽤原⽂：Sometimes Bostonians seem like they're just too busy to be polite.\nC) 原词陷阱\nviolate traffic rules more frequently 盗用第一段后半罗列的现象——pedestrians\nwalking when the crosswalk lights say not to、car drivers speeding，但这些是说话人\n列举的所见，不是 one study 的结论，属原词陷阱。\n借⽤原⽂：car drivers speeding, making a loud noise with the horn\nD) 过度推断\nmore easily irritated in public 由后文 cursing、rude 的氛围引申而来，但研究句并未说\n波士顿人\"更易动怒\"，是顺着语气过度推断。\n借⽤原⽂：occasionally cursing at each other", "points": ["听力·讲座", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2025-06-1-l-24", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 24, "stem": "What does Thomas O'Connor say about the idea of strangers greeting one another?", "options": ["A) It was practiced by Boston's founding fathers.", "B) It is not deemed exotic by Proper Bostonians.", "C) It was adopted by Boston's upper class.", "D) It is not part of Boston's local culture."], "answer": "D", "analysis": "【答案】D （细节题） It is not part of Boston's local culture.\n【定位】The idea of a stranger greeting another stranger is simply not part of the local culture, he says.\n他说，陌生人跟陌生人打招呼这种事，根本就不属于本地文化。\n【信号】he says 归属信号。这句以 he says 收尾，he 回指本段正在论述的 Thomas O'Connor，\n把观点准确归到他名下；其内容直接回答\"陌生人打招呼\"与文化的关系，是答案句。\n【替换】simply not part of the local culture ↔ It is not part of Boston's local culture.\nnear-原句对应\n【排除】A) 张冠李戴\npracticed by Boston's founding fathers 盗用了 the Puritan founding fathers，但那是\nChester Atkins 的说法、且讲的是性情急躁，与 O'Connor 谈\"陌生人打招呼\"无关，属张冠\n李戴。\n借⽤原⽂：the Puritan founding fathers were themselves impatient and bad-tempered\nB) 原词陷阱\nnot deemed exotic by Proper Bostonians 把 Proper Bostonians 这个原词拼到不存在的\nexotic 上，Proper Bostonians 出自 Whitehead 谈\"以礼仪划阶级\"的论述，原文从无\"是否\n异域风\"之说，属原词陷阱。\n借⽤原⽂：Proper Bostonians made other people scornful of courtesy\nC) 张冠李戴\nadopted by Boston's upper class 盗用 Boston's upper class used manners to make\nclass distinctions，那是 Whitehead 的说法、讲的是上层用礼仪划分阶级，并非\nO'Connor 论\"陌生人打招呼\"，属张冠李戴。\n借⽤原⽂：Boston's upper class used manners to make class distinctions", "points": ["听力·讲座", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2025-06-1-l-25", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 25, "stem": "What does the speaker think Boston should do?", "options": ["A) Stick to its own way of showing courtesy to strangers.", "B) Follow the examples set by Paris and New York City.", "C) Learn from the world's major cities in promoting tourism.", "D) Take pride in its history and adhere to its cultural tradition."], "answer": "B", "analysis": "【答案】B （推断题） Follow the examples set by Paris and New York City.\n【定位】Paris is one example where people are trying to be friendly. New York City is another. There,\nthe city is trying to persuade subway riders to be more considerate. If those cities can make\nthe effort, maybe Boston should as well.\n巴黎就是一个例子，那里的人在努力变得友善。纽约也是，那座城市正设法说服地铁乘客多\n为他人着想。如果这些城市都能努力，那么也许波士顿也应该这么做。\n【信号】末句 maybe…should 表态。结尾 If those cities can make the effort, maybe Boston\nshould as well. 用条件句含蓄给出说话人的建议——既然巴黎、纽约都在努力改，波士顿\n也应仿效，是承载态度的答案句。\n【替换】Paris is one example ↔ Follow the examples set by Paris and New York City.\n举例即榜样\nmaybe Boston should as well ↔ Follow the examples\nshould as well=该照做\n【排除】A) 与原文相反\nStick to its own way of showing courtesy to strangers 是\"保持原样\"，恰与说话人\"也该\n努力改\"的劝告相反；它呼应的是上文 the city will never change，但那是\"some say\"的观\n点、被 Still 一转否掉了。\n借⽤原⽂：Some say that's just the way Boston is and that the city will never change.\nC) 未提及\nLearn from the world's major cities in promoting tourism 借用了\"向大城市学\"的影子，\n但原文谈的是变友善、有礼，从未提\"promoting tourism（推广旅游）\"，属未提及。\nD) 与原文相反\nTake pride in its history and adhere to its cultural tradition 盗用前文 Boston's history\n等词，主张\"守住历史与传统\"，与说话人\"该像巴黎纽约一样去改\"正好相反，属与原文相\n反。\n借⽤原⽂：Boston's history as a city where each new group of immigrants was treated\nwith suspicion", "points": ["听力·讲座", "推断题"]}, {"id": "2025-06-1-c-26", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 26, "stem": "", "options": ["A) aesthetic", "B) chronic", "C) contaminated", "D) conventionally", "E) emissions", "F) environment", "G) hampering", "H) incidentally", "I) infringement", "J) intrigued", "K) juvenile", "L) outlet", "M) rotating", "N) vibrations", "O) weeding"], "answer": "J", "analysis": "【答案】J) intrigued\n形容词\n【词性槽】be 动词后作表语\n【依据】发现新天地→产生兴趣\n上文 discovered a small but growing movement 表明她们发现了一个新兴运动；They\nwere __(26)__ 紧承其后，自然描述她们对这场运动**产生浓厚兴趣**的状态。be intrigued\n是描述人被某事吸引的经典搭配。\n【竞争词】A) aesthetic '她们是美学的'语义不通\naesthetic 修饰物的审美属性，不用于描述人的情绪反应，'They were aesthetic'语义荒谬。\nC) contaminated '她们被污染了'语义不合\ncontaminated 修饰水/土等物质或抽象意义的'被污染的（思想）'，描述这群积极投身环保的学\n生，方向完全相反。\nK) juvenile 幼稚的与积极行动矛盾\njuvenile 含贬义，会把这群有担当的大学生贬为'幼稚'，与全段她们果决行动的基调冲突。", "points": ["选词填空", "词性判断·be 动词后作表语"]}, {"id": "2025-06-1-c-27", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 27, "stem": "", "options": ["A) aesthetic", "B) chronic", "C) contaminated", "D) conventionally", "E) emissions", "F) environment", "G) hampering", "H) incidentally", "I) infringement", "J) intrigued", "K) juvenile", "L) outlet", "M) rotating", "N) vibrations", "O) weeding"], "answer": "B", "analysis": "【答案】B) chronic\n形容词\n【词性槽】不定冠词与名词之间作定语\n【依据】气候焦虑是长期慢性的\n破折号后是对 climate anxiety 的同位解释。气候焦虑本质上是一种**长期、持续**的恐\n惧，chronic fear（慢性恐惧）是心理学常用搭配，正好刻画这种持续不消退的状态，与\n'climate anxiety' 的本义对应。\n【竞争词】A) aesthetic 审美的不能修饰 fear\naesthetic 指审美/美学的，与恐惧情绪无逻辑关系，搭配不成立。\nC) contaminated 被污染的不修饰情绪\ncontaminated 用于水、土壤等被污染的物质，不能修饰 fear 这种抽象情绪。\nJ) intrigued 感兴趣的修饰人不修饰 fear\nintrigued 表'被深深吸引的'，主语通常是人，且与恐惧情绪在情感色彩上相反。\nK) juvenile 幼稚的与气候焦虑色彩冲突\njuvenile 表'少年的/幼稚的'，带贬义，会把气候焦虑矮化为'孩子气的恐惧'，与下文积极行动的\n语调相悖。", "points": ["选词填空", "词性判断·不定冠词与名词之间作定语"]}, {"id": "2025-06-1-c-28", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 28, "stem": "", "options": ["A) aesthetic", "B) chronic", "C) contaminated", "D) conventionally", "E) emissions", "F) environment", "G) hampering", "H) incidentally", "I) infringement", "J) intrigued", "K) juvenile", "L) outlet", "M) rotating", "N) vibrations", "O) weeding"], "answer": "L", "analysis": "【答案】L) outlet\n名词\n【词性槽】不定冠词 an 后缺单数名词\n【依据】气候焦虑需要情绪出口\n上文 Charlene experiences climate anxiety—a __(27)__ fear of a climate catastrophe\n说她有气候焦虑，下文 she felt she \"had found a specific area to focus on\" 表明她找到\n了可投入精力的具体领域——这正是焦虑情绪的**释放出口**（outlet）。look for an\noutlet 是描述情绪宣泄的固定搭配。\n【竞争词】I) infringement 侵权不是被'寻找'的对象\nlook for an infringement（寻找一项侵权行为）在语义上荒谬，且与 climate anxiety 的语境\n完全脱节。\nN) vibrations 辅音开头不能跟 an\nvibrations 以辅音 /v/ 开头，先被 an 拒之门外；且词义与气候焦虑无关。", "points": ["选词填空", "词性判断·不定冠词 an 后缺单数名词"]}, {"id": "2025-06-1-c-29", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 29, "stem": "", "options": ["A) aesthetic", "B) chronic", "C) contaminated", "D) conventionally", "E) emissions", "F) environment", "G) hampering", "H) incidentally", "I) infringement", "J) intrigued", "K) juvenile", "L) outlet", "M) rotating", "N) vibrations", "O) weeding"], "answer": "F", "analysis": "【答案】F) environment\n名词\n【词性槽】形容词串后缺名词中心词\n【依据】三形容词指向'环境'\nhealthier、safer、cleaner 是描述**生存环境**的高频搭配三件套，a healthier, safer,\ncleaner __(29)__ for all 整句承接上文 fighting for，正是为所有人争取一个更健康、安\n全、洁净的**环境**，与全文反对除草剂、保护校园生态的主旨完全吻合。\n【竞争词】E) emissions 排放物不能被'更健康'修饰\nemissions 指废气排放物，搭配 healthier/safer/cleaner 在语义上矛盾——人们要的是减少排\n放，而不是'更健康的排放'。\nI) infringement 侵权与三形容词南辕北辙\ninfringement 指对权利的侵犯，是负面行为，无法用 healthier/safer/cleaner 来修饰，且与本\n文话题无关。\nL) outlet 释放渠道与'更健康'不搭\noutlet 指情绪/精力的发泄出口，是个体层面的概念，不能用 healthier/safer/cleaner for all 这\n种宏观三连修饰。\nN) vibrations 振动与文章话题无关\nvibrations 指物理振动，与气候、校园生态无关，纯粹凑数的干扰项。", "points": ["选词填空", "词性判断·形容词串后缺名词中心词"]}, {"id": "2025-06-1-c-30", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 30, "stem": "", "options": ["A) aesthetic", "B) chronic", "C) contaminated", "D) conventionally", "E) emissions", "F) environment", "G) hampering", "H) incidentally", "I) infringement", "J) intrigued", "K) juvenile", "L) outlet", "M) rotating", "N) vibrations", "O) weeding"], "answer": "A", "analysis": "【答案】A) aesthetic\n形容词\n【词性槽】名词 goals 前作定语\n【依据】美观目标对应下文绿色草坪\n下文 Having a \"beautiful\" campus means green and perfectly maintained lawns\nalong with flower beds and paved sidewalks 立刻解释了所谓 'goals' 就是把校园弄得\n**美观漂亮**——这正是 aesthetic（审美/美观）目标。命题人用引号 \"beautiful\" 直接回\n扣 aesthetic。\n【竞争词】C) contaminated 被污染的目标语义颠倒\ncontaminated goals（被污染的目标）语义不通；高校追求的不可能是'被污染'目标，方向\n反了。\nK) juvenile 幼稚目标与高校追求不符\njuvenile goals 含贬义，高校公开追求'幼稚目标'语义荒诞，与下文美观校园语境无关。", "points": ["选词填空", "词性判断·名词 goals 前作定语"]}, {"id": "2025-06-1-c-31", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 31, "stem": "", "options": ["A) aesthetic", "B) chronic", "C) contaminated", "D) conventionally", "E) emissions", "F) environment", "G) hampering", "H) incidentally", "I) infringement", "J) intrigued", "K) juvenile", "L) outlet", "M) rotating", "N) vibrations", "O) weeding"], "answer": "D", "analysis": "【答案】D) conventionally\n副词\n【词性槽】形容词 managed 前作状语\n【依据】传统方式=合成农药管理\n上文 Most institutions of higher education rely on synthetic pesticides and fertilizers\n说大多数高校沿用合成农药——这正是**传统/常规**管理方式；these __(31)__ managed\ncampuses 中的 these 回指前句，因此填 conventionally（按传统方式）正好呼应。\n【竞争词】H) incidentally 顺便地与代价主句无关\nincidentally 意为'顺便提一句、偶然地'，常作插入语，无法刻画'校园被怎样管理'的方式；与下\n文 'come at a cost' 的因果论证脱节。\n【易错】最具迷惑性的错误词是 H) —— 副词只剩两个，色彩不挑就翻车。31 题词性确定后只剩\nconventionally 和 incidentally 两选一。考生若只看词性、不回头读上文 Most\ninstitutions of higher education rely on synthetic pesticides and fertilizers 的'常规手\n段'铺垫，就可能误选 incidentally。防坑：副词空二选一时，必回上文找语义铺垫。", "points": ["选词填空", "词性判断·形容词 managed 前作状语"]}, {"id": "2025-06-1-c-32", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 32, "stem": "", "options": ["A) aesthetic", "B) chronic", "C) contaminated", "D) conventionally", "E) emissions", "F) environment", "G) hampering", "H) incidentally", "I) infringement", "J) intrigued", "K) juvenile", "L) outlet", "M) rotating", "N) vibrations", "O) weeding"], "answer": "C", "analysis": "【答案】C) contaminated\n形容词\n【词性槽】名词 waterways 前作定语\n【依据】并列同义堆叠负面后果\nincreased cancer risk, __(32)__ waterways, poisoned wildlife and lifeless soil 是命题人\n列出的四宗罪：增加癌症风险、__水道、毒害野生动物、贫瘠土壤。waterways 与化学品代\n价最自然的搭配就是 contaminated（被污染的），与 poisoned/lifeless 一同构成负面后\n果链。\n【竞争词】K) juvenile 幼稚的水道语义不通\njuvenile waterways（幼稚的水道）搭配不成立，且不属于负面后果范畴。\n【易错】最具迷惑性的错误词是 K) —— 形容词色彩一错全错。32 题在并列负面后果 poisoned\nwildlife、lifeless soil 之间，考生若只挑形容词词性不察色彩，可能把 juvenile 硬塞进去。\n防坑：并列项前的修饰词必须与左右邻居在感情色彩上一致。", "points": ["选词填空", "词性判断·名词 waterways 前作定语"]}, {"id": "2025-06-1-c-33", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 33, "stem": "", "options": ["A) aesthetic", "B) chronic", "C) contaminated", "D) conventionally", "E) emissions", "F) environment", "G) hampering", "H) incidentally", "I) infringement", "J) intrigued", "K) juvenile", "L) outlet", "M) rotating", "N) vibrations", "O) weeding"], "answer": "E", "analysis": "【答案】E) emissions\n名词\n【词性槽】形容词后作宾语缺名词复数\n【依据】石油成分对应温室气体排放\nas they can include petroleum-based ingredients 明示这些化学品含石油基成分，正是\n温室气体排放（emissions）的来源；上文 Pesticide use on college campuses also\ncontributes to our global climate crisis 也铺垫了气候话题，indirect emissions 即'间接\n排放'是气候议题的标准术语。\n【竞争词】I) infringement 侵权与石油成分无逻辑链\ninfringement 指权利侵犯，无法解释为何'含石油成分'会导致它增加，与气候危机话题完全\n无关。\nL) outlet 发泄渠道与排放概念错位\noutlet 虽有'出口'义，但常指情感/能量的发泄渠道或电源插座，不用于描述温室气体的间接产\n生，与 petroleum-based ingredients 接不上。\nN) vibrations 振动与石油成分无关\nvibrations 指物理振动，与气候危机、石油成分无任何因果链。", "points": ["选词填空", "词性判断·形容词后作宾语缺名词复数"]}, {"id": "2025-06-1-c-34", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 34, "stem": "", "options": ["A) aesthetic", "B) chronic", "C) contaminated", "D) conventionally", "E) emissions", "F) environment", "G) hampering", "H) incidentally", "I) infringement", "J) intrigued", "K) juvenile", "L) outlet", "M) rotating", "N) vibrations", "O) weeding"], "answer": "G", "analysis": "【答案】G) hampering\n动词\n【词性槽】并列分词作伴随状语\n【依据】削弱土壤功能=妨碍其能力\ndecreases the life in soil, __(34)__ the ability of soils to absorb carbon or retain\nwater，主句说减少土壤生命，分词层进一步说**妨碍**土壤吸收碳/保水的能力；\nhampering（妨碍）与 reducing（减少）在 and 前后形成同义递进，语义、形态都对齐。\n【竞争词】M) rotating 旋转土壤能力语义不通\nrotating the ability of soils（旋转土壤能力）搭配不成立；rotating 通常用于轮作（rotating\ncrops）等，与本句负面后果链脱节。", "points": ["选词填空", "词性判断·并列分词作伴随状语"]}, {"id": "2025-06-1-c-35", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 35, "stem": "", "options": ["A) aesthetic", "B) chronic", "C) contaminated", "D) conventionally", "E) emissions", "F) environment", "G) hampering", "H) incidentally", "I) infringement", "J) intrigued", "K) juvenile", "L) outlet", "M) rotating", "N) vibrations", "O) weeding"], "answer": "O", "analysis": "【答案】O) weeding\n动词\n【词性槽】with 后接动名词作宾语\n【依据】替代农药=人工除草\n上文 Instead of using toxic chemicals 明示是有毒化学品的**替代方案**——既然不喷除\n草剂，那学生帮忙做的就是**人工除草**（weeding）。weed the campus grounds（给\n校园场地除草）也是该词的典型搭配。\n【竞争词】G) hampering 妨碍校园场地语义颠倒\nhampering the campus grounds（妨碍校园场地）语义荒谬，且学生'帮忙'不可能是'妨碍'，与\nhelp out 的色彩反向。\nM) rotating 轮作场地与替代农药无关\nrotating the campus grounds（旋转/轮换校园场地）语义牵强，与上文'不用化学药品'的对照\n逻辑接不上。", "points": ["选词填空", "词性判断·with 后接动名词作宾语"]}, {"id": "2025-06-1-m-36", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 36, "stem": "People going to the library in search of information can build connections with each other there.", "options": null, "answer": "G", "analysis": "【答案】G 段（强锚点）\n【锚点】找信息却建立彼此联系\n【定位】In the words of Robert Putnam, \"People may go to the library looking mainly for information,\nbut they find each other there.\"\n用罗伯特·普特南的话说：\"人们去图书馆主要是为了寻找信息，但他们在那里彼此相遇。\"\n【改写】People going to the library in search of information ↔\nPeople may go to the library looking mainly for information\n\"寻找信息\"原样同义\ncan build connections with each other there ↔ they find each other there\n\"找到彼此\"=\"建立连接\"", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2025-06-1-m-37", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 37, "stem": "According to advocates of libraries as community builders, librarians are not doing their job well if they do not change their communities.", "options": null, "answer": "C", "analysis": "【答案】C 段（强锚点）\n【锚点】倡导者 + 不改变社区即未尽职\n【定位】Supporters of libraries as community builders claim that unless you are out there changing\nneighborhoods, you are not completing the work you are to do.\n支持图书馆作为社区建设者的倡导者声称：除非你走出去改变所在社区，否则你就没有完成\n你该做的工作。\n【改写】advocates of libraries as community builders ↔ Supporters of libraries as community builders\nadvocates ↔ Supporters 同义\nlibrarians are not doing their job well if they do not change their communities ↔\nunless you are out there changing neighborhoods, you are not completing the work you are to\ndo\nunless 双否定 ↔ if not 肯定条件\n【辨邻】 B B 段虽然出现 community builders 这个标签，但没有任何\"不改变社区就没做好工\n作\"的主张，缺第二层指纹。\nSome of the specific ways in which libraries add value to our communities and serve as cultural\ncenters for our patrons are community builders, centers for the arts, and champions of youth.", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2025-06-1-m-38", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 38, "stem": "With the costs of education continually rising, public libraries remain places where all people can have access to education.", "options": null, "answer": "I", "analysis": "【答案】I 段（强锚点）\n【锚点】教育成本上升 + 人人可获取教育\n【定位】In a time when education is increasingly expensive, public libraries provide information and\neducational opportunities free for all people, regardless of their socio-economic status.\n在教育越来越昂贵的时代，公共图书馆免费向所有人提供信息与教育机会，无论其社会经济\n地位如何。\n【改写】With the costs of education continually rising ↔\nIn a time when education is increasingly expensive\n\"成本上升\"=\"越来越贵\"名词化重构\npublic libraries remain places where all people can have access to education ↔\npublic libraries provide information and educational opportunities free for all people,\nregardless of their socio-economic status\n\"人人有教育机会\"原样同义\n【辨邻】 H H 段谈的是 concerts, author visits, and gallery displays 这种文化活动免费，不是\neducation；少了\"教育越来越贵\"的前提，整句对不上。\npublic library events (including concerts, author visits, and gallery displays) are often offered free\nof charge, enabling people of any income level to attend\nL L 段聚焦 students 与 tutoring, homework help programs, and summer reading\nprograms，未提及\"教育成本不断上升\"作为整体前提，且适用人群是学生不是所\n有人。\nFree tutoring, homework help programs, and summer reading programs for kids and teens help\nbridge the economic divide that impacts students' academic performance", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2025-06-1-m-39", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 39, "stem": "Libraries draw large crowds, thus creating lots of business opportunities in neighboring areas.", "options": null, "answer": "D", "analysis": "【答案】D 段（强锚点）\n【锚点】吸引人流 + 周边商机\n【定位】Like a major department store in a mall, libraries attract large numbers of people, creating\neconomic opportunities for numerous businesses and organizations in the surrounding area.\n就像商场里的主力百货公司一样，图书馆吸引大量人流，为周边的众多企业和机构创造经济\n机会。\n【改写】Libraries draw large crowds ↔ libraries attract large numbers of people\ndraw=attract，crowds=large numbers of people\ncreating lots of business opportunities in neighboring areas ↔\ncreating economic opportunities for numerous businesses and organizations in the\nsurrounding area\nbusiness=economic for businesses，neighboring=surrounding", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2025-06-1-m-40", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 40, "stem": "With the world more and more digitalized, people sometimes underestimate the role of libraries as community and cultural centers.", "options": null, "answer": "A", "analysis": "【答案】A 段（强锚点）\n【锚点】世界数字化 + 角色被低估\n【定位】But in an increasingly digital world, we see the role of libraries as community and cultural\ncenters at times undervalued.\n但是，在一个日益数字化的世界里，我们有时会看到图书馆作为社区与文化中心的角色被\n低估。\n【改写】With the world more and more digitalized ↔ in an increasingly digital world\n\"日益数字化\"原样同义\npeople sometimes underestimate the role of libraries as community and cultural centers ↔\nwe see the role of libraries as community and cultural centers at times undervalued\nunderestimate=undervalued，sometimes=at times", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2025-06-1-m-41", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 41, "stem": "Various programs organized by public libraries for children and adolescents help narrow the gap between students from varying economic backgrounds.", "options": null, "answer": "L", "analysis": "【答案】L 段（强锚点）\n【锚点】面向儿童青少年的项目 + 缩小经济差距\n【定位】Free tutoring, homework help programs, and summer reading programs for kids and teens\nhelp bridge the economic divide that impacts students' academic performance.\n面向少年儿童的免费辅导、作业辅导项目以及暑期阅读项目，帮助弥合那条影响学生学业表\n现的经济鸿沟。\n【改写】Various programs organized by public libraries for children and adolescents ↔\nFree tutoring, homework help programs, and summer reading programs for kids and teens\n上位词 programs 总括多个具体项目\nnarrow the gap between students from varying economic backgrounds ↔\nbridge the economic divide that impacts students' academic performance\nnarrow=bridge，gap=divide\n【辨邻】 M M 段聚焦 children learn to share, to be engaged in their communities, to\nparticipate in the arts，与题干\"缩小经济差距\"无对应。\nThrough library collections, programs, and physical spaces, children learn to share, to be engaged\nin their communities, to participate in the arts, and to explore their immediate world and the world\nat large.", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2025-06-1-m-42", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 42, "stem": "In an organized archive, people can do research on their family history and find out how their ancestors came to settle in the new land.", "options": null, "answer": "F", "analysis": "【答案】F 段（强锚点）\n【锚点】档案 + 家族史 + 祖辈如何来到新土地\n【定位】An organized archive is a place where people can research their ancestry and immigration\nhistory, do environmental research, and more.\n一个组织有序的档案馆，是人们可以研究自己的祖辈血统与移民史、做环境研究等的地方。\n【改写】In an organized archive ↔ An organized archive is a place\n原样复现\npeople can do research on their family history ↔ people can research their ancestry\nfamily history=ancestry\nfind out how their ancestors came to settle in the new land ↔ immigration history\n\"移民史\"= 祖辈如何来到新土地", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2025-06-1-m-43", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 43, "stem": "Public libraries organize cultural events, often allowing people of different income levels to attend free of charge.", "options": null, "answer": "H", "analysis": "【答案】H 段（强锚点）\n【锚点】文化活动 + 不同收入层次免费参与\n【定位】Despite the rising costs of concert and theater tickets, public library events (including\nconcerts, author visits, and gallery displays) are often offered free of charge, enabling people\nof any income level to attend.\n尽管音乐会和剧院门票越来越贵，公共图书馆举办的活动（包括音乐会、作家见面会和画廊\n展览）却常常免费提供，让任何收入水平的人都能参加。\n【改写】Public libraries organize cultural events ↔\npublic library events (including concerts, author visits, and gallery displays)\ncultural events 上位概括\nallowing people of different income levels to attend free of charge ↔\noffered free of charge, enabling people of any income level to attend\nallowing=enabling，different=any income level\n【辨邻】 I I 段谈的是 provide information and educational opportunities free for all\npeople，与题干 cultural events 对象不一致。\npublic libraries provide information and educational opportunities free for all people, regardless of\ntheir socio-economic status", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2025-06-1-m-44", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 44, "stem": "Besides being an information provider, the library performs many other important services for the community.", "options": null, "answer": "B", "analysis": "【答案】B 段（弱锚点）\n【锚点】信息提供者之外的多重服务\n【定位】More than just books and banks of computers, libraries are still places where individuals\ngather to explore, interact, and imagine.\n图书馆不仅是书籍与成排电脑的所在，仍是人们聚集起来探索、互动与想象的地方。\n【改写】Besides being an information provider ↔ More than just books and banks of computers\nbesides=more than just，两种总览结构互替\nthe library performs many other important services for the community ↔\nLibraries serve in these capacities and are more than just about information\nperforms many other services=serve in these capacities + more than information\n【辨邻】 A A 段 we see the role of libraries as community and cultural centers at times\nundervalued 谈的是评价（低估），而非\"列举信息以外的多种服务\"。\npublic library services that focus on building communities face-to-face, inspiring and educating\npatrons about art, literature, and music, and helping patrons engage in civil discourse can seem\nold-fashioned\nN N 段 These examples are just a few of the many amazing things that public\nlibraries around the United States (and the world) are doing to build and\nmaintain strong community connections 落点是\"鼓励复制 + 说服领导\"，与题干的\n总览主张不一致。\nWe encourage you to try some of these ideas in your own libraries, and we hope that these ideas\nwill help you be better able to convince your community leaders of the important role that public\nlibraries play in communities large and small.", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2025-06-1-m-45", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 45, "stem": "Public libraries can help build small communities of people with different backgrounds and ideologies.", "options": null, "answer": "K", "analysis": "【答案】K 段（强锚点）\n【锚点】不同背景与意识形态的小型社区\n【定位】Public libraries, through such programs as The Human Library and Socrates Café, can help\nbuild small communities of difference that encourage people to interact with and learn from\neach other through dialogue.\n公共图书馆通过 The Human Library 和 Socrates Café 等项目，能够帮助构建由差异组成\n的小型社区，鼓励人们通过对话相互交流、相互学习。\n【改写】build small communities of people with different backgrounds and ideologies ↔\nbuild small communities of difference\nof difference 展开为 of people with different ...\nPublic libraries can help ↔\nPublic libraries, through such programs as The Human Library and Socrates Café, can help\n原样复现\n【辨邻】 G G 段 community-building connections are happening all the time 强调的是\"找信\n息 → 找到彼此\"的反差，与题干\"不同背景与意识形态\"的限定不一致。\nNew moms connect at baby story-times; elderly people, often facing difficult life transitions, attend\nevents and find that they make new friends; teenagers meet up in libraries' teen spaces after school;\nand readers discuss current events in the periodicals room.\nPARAGRAPH MAP · 段落主旨图\n个段落各讲了什么\n逐段一句主旨 + 该段最具区分度的「信息指纹」，是逐句回扫定位的工作台。\nA 提出全文论点：在日益数字化与预算缩减的背景下，图书馆作为社区与文化中心的角\n色被低估，但恰恰因此变得比以往更重要。\n信息指纹 数字化时代角色被低估\nB 总览段：图书馆不只是书与电脑，还是社区建设者、艺术中心、青少年守护者，承担\n信息以外的多重价值。\n信息指纹 信息之外的多重身份总览\nC\n讲图书馆作为社区建设者的角色，并引述支持者观点：不走出去改变社区，就没做好\n工作。\n信息指纹 不改变社区即未完成工作\nD\n以\"基于地点的经济发展\"切入，把图书馆类比为商场里的主力百货，能吸引人流、为\n周边企业带来商机。\n信息指纹 图书馆如主力百货带动周边商机\nE\n讲图书馆推动城市可持续性，举例本地农夫市集与种子借阅图书馆等环保与本地经济\n实践。\n信息指纹 可持续与本地食物 / 种子借阅\nF\n讲档案馆作用：保存口述史、数字化档案，帮助人们研究家族史与移民史。\n信息指纹 档案馆与家族 / 移民史研究\nG 引用罗伯特·普特南的话：人们到图书馆找信息，却在那里找到了彼此，举新妈妈、老\n人、青少年、读者的例子说明社区连接。\n信息指纹 找信息却找到彼此\nH 引用基思·理查兹的话\"公共图书馆是伟大的平等器\"：演出和讲座往往免费，让不同收\n入层次的人都能参与。\n信息指纹 伟大的平等器 / 免费文化活动\nI 在教育日益昂贵的时代，公共图书馆免费向所有人提供信息与教育机会，举 American\nLibrary Association 的 Let's Talk about It 项目为例。\n信息指纹 教育昂贵 / 免费向所有人开放\nJ 强调读者也是\"信息生产者\"：图书馆为想做维基百科、博客、播客、杂志、艺术与音\n乐创作的人提供空间与服务。\n信息指纹 读者亦是信息生产者\nK 讲公民对话的衰落与图书馆的补救：通过 The Human Library、Socrates Café 等项\n目，把不同背景与意识形态的人聚到一起对话。\n信息指纹 不同背景与意识形态的小型社区\nL 免费辅导、作业辅导与暑期阅读项目帮助弥合影响学生学业表现的经济鸿沟。\n信息指纹 弥合学生经济鸿沟的辅导与暑期阅读\nM\n讲图书馆面向儿童的服务：通过馆藏、项目与空间让孩子学会分享、参与社区与艺\n术，举 Nature Explorium 为例。\n信息指纹 面向儿童 / Nature Explorium\nN\n结尾段：鼓励读者把这些做法引入自家图书馆，并向社区领导者证明公共图书馆的重\n要角色。\n信息指纹 鼓励复制 / 说服社区领导", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2025-06-1-r-46", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 46, "stem": "What does the U.S. secretary of education say in his open letter?", "options": ["A) It is of vital importance to respond promptly to the school absence crisis.", "B) The academic performance of chronically absent students is deplorable.", "C) Low academic achievement is mainly attributed to school absences.", "D) The effect of school absences on American education is worrisome."], "answer": "C", "analysis": "【答案】C （细节题）\n【判型】What does the U.S. secretary of education say in his open letter? 问的是某人具体说的\n内容，答案就在原文某句话里，不需推断。\n【拆句】结构是 What does the U.S. secretary of education say in his open letter?——典型\nwhat 引导的特殊疑问句，主语 the U.S. secretary of education，谓语 say，介词短语 in\nhis open letter 限定 say 的发生场景；无从句、无否定、无陷阱。\n【定位】His open letter stated that missing 10% of school days in a year for any reason—excused or\nunexcused—is a primary cause of low academic achievement.\n他的公开信指出，一年缺课 10%——无论是否有故——是低学业成就的一个主要原因。\n【选项】A) 过度推断\n公开信里没有任何关于 \"必须迅速回应\" 的话——urging communities to support\nevery student 是劝学校社区支持学生出勤，不是对 respond promptly 的呼吁；A 项\n把作者（urging）的动作改写成信件内容，且加了原文没有的 \"vital importance /\npromptly\" 程度词。\nB) 偷换概念\ndeplored 在原文里描述的是 Education officials 对 \"all this missed instruction\" 的态\n度，B 项把它偷换成了 \"chronically absent students 的 academic performance is\ndeplorable\"——主语从 \"缺课现象\" 偷换成 \"学生的学业表现\"，evaluatee 错位。\nC) ✓ 正确\nmissing 10% of school days in a year for any reason ↔ school absences\nis a primary cause of low academic achievement ↔\nLow academic achievement is mainly attributed to\nC 项把 missing 10% of school days … is a primary cause of low academic\nachievement 的主动因果倒装为 \"低学业主要归因于缺课\" 的被动因果，与预判 \"缺课是\n学业差的主因\" 完全吻合。\nD) 张冠李戴\n公开信原文用 primary cause 的客观因果定性，并未对 \"school absences 对美国教育\n的影响\" 作 worrisome 这种情感色彩评价——D 项把 Worrying 段的句首词搬到这里，\n但那是作者对 \"担心缺勤\" 的转述，并非教育部长公开信的内容。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2025-06-1-r-47", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 47, "stem": "What do the authors find about school absences?", "options": ["A) Excused school absences have little impact on children's learning.", "B) There is little difference between unexcused and excused absences.", "C) Excused absences lead to comparatively better school performance.", "D) Unexcused absences are a big challenge to both schools and families."], "answer": "A", "analysis": "【答案】A （细节题）\n【判型】What do the authors find about school absences? 问的是作者的客观发现，原文必有对\n应陈述句，不属于推断。\n【拆句】结构是 What do the authors find about school absences?——What 作 find 的宾语，\n主语 the authors，介词短语 about school absences 限定 find 的话题范围；句法简单，\n没有陷阱。\n【定位】We find that absences excused by a parent do little to harm children's learning.\n我们发现，由家长请假的 excused absences 对孩子的学习几乎没有伤害。\n【选项】A) ✓ 正确\nabsences excused by a parent ↔ Excused school absences\ndo little to harm children's learning ↔ have little impact on children's learning\nA 项是 P6 第一句的字面同义改写——主语 excused absences、谓语 do little to\nharm 全部一一对应，无任何添加或扭曲，与预判完全吻合。\nB) 与原文相反\n原文 the average child with even just one unexcused absence does much worse\nacademically than peers with none 明确指出 unexcused 与 excused 差异很大——B\n项 little difference 与原文 much worse 直接相反。\nC) 过度推断\n原文只说 excused absences do little to harm、有 excused 缺勤的孩子 have test\nscores equal to their peers who have no absences——是 \"equal to\"（相当），不\n是 comparatively better（相对更好）。\"持平\" 被悄悄抬升为 \"更好\"。\nD) 答非所问\nUnexcused absences are a big challenge to both schools and families 谈的是\nunexcused 对学校和家庭构成挑战——题干 Q47 问的是 \"authors 关于 school\nabsences 的发现\"，作者的核心发现是 excused 不损学（P6），而非 unexcused 对学\n校家庭有多挑战。话题偏离。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2025-06-1-r-48", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 48, "stem": "What do the authors believe concerning unexcused school absences?", "options": ["A) They are likely to cause a decrease in students' academic achievements due to missed instruction.", "B) They point directly to many of the out-of-school challenges confronting children and their families.", "C) They are matters the American government typically ignores when formulating educational policies.", "D) They give a clear signal to children and their families of the crises they are likely to face in the future."], "answer": "B", "analysis": "【答案】B （细节题）\n【判型】What do the authors believe concerning unexcused school absences? 问的是作者已\n经明说的观点，原文有原句，属于细节题。\n【拆句】结构是 What do the authors believe concerning unexcused school absences?——\nconcerning 等于 about，是介词，引出讨论对象；与 Q47 唯一的差异在限定词\nunexcused，读题时务必圈出。\n【定位】We believe unexcused absence is a strong signal of the many challenges children and families\nface, including economic and medical hardships.\n我们认为，无故缺勤是孩子和家庭所面临诸多挑战的强烈信号，包括经济与医疗方面的\n困难。\n【选项】A) 与原文相反\nThey are likely to cause a decrease in students' academic achievements due to\nmissed instruction 是作者明确反对的观点——It matters less as a cause of lower\nstudent achievement due to missed instruction 中 \"matters less as a cause\" 直接\n否定了 \"缺课通过 missed instruction 导致学业下降\" 这一因果。A 项把作者反对的观\n点扣给作者本人。\nB) ✓ 正确\nis a strong signal of ↔ point directly to\nthe many challenges children and families face ↔\nmany of the out-of-school challenges confronting children and their families\nB 项把 is a strong signal of 改写为 point directly to——\"信号\" 与 \"指向\" 是六级常见\n的隐喻互换，与预判 \"无故缺勤指向校外挑战\" 完全吻合。\nC) 无中生有\n原文未说政府 typically ignores unexcused absences——恰恰相反，P10 说政策\nhold schools and families accountable for the days children miss，是 \"管太严\" 而\n非 \"忽视\"。C 项凭空捏造 \"政府忽视\" 这一事实。\nD) 偷换概念\n原文 is a strong signal of the many challenges children and families face 用一般现\n在时 face，指 \"当下面对\" 的挑战；D 项改成 the crises they are likely to face in the\nfuture 把时间挪到未来——把 \"反映当下危机\" 偷换成 \"预测未来危机\"。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2025-06-1-r-49", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 49, "stem": "What is the assumption underlying education policies in the U.S.?", "options": ["A) Children's academic performance depends on reducing the number of absences.", "B) Schools can boost children's academic performance by effective intervention.", "C) Schools as well as families should be held responsible for out-of-school crises.", "D) Children's academic performance is closely related to the quality of instruction."], "answer": "B", "analysis": "【答案】B （细节题）\n【判型】What is the assumption underlying education policies in the U.S.? 问的是原文已经写\n明的预设，不是要读者推断政策背后的隐含逻辑。\n【拆句】结构是 What is the assumption underlying education policies in the U.S.?——主语是\nthe assumption，underlying 是现在分词作后置定语（= that underlies），限定\nassumption 是 \"作为政策基础的\" 那种预设；不要把 underlying 误读为时间状语。\n【定位】They also assume that schools will be able to effectively intervene by reducing\nstudent absences.\n这些政策还预设，学校能通过减少学生缺勤来有效进行干预。\n【选项】A) 偷换概念\n原文 missing school for any reason harms children academically 是 \"缺课损学\n业\"——A 项 Children's academic performance depends on reducing the number\nof absences 把它倒过来说 \"学业取决于减少缺勤\"，表面相近但因果主语错位：原文主\n语是 \"缺课\"，A 项主语变成 \"学业\"，且 depends on 把单向因果偷换为 \"取决于\" 这一\n更强的依存关系。\nB) ✓ 正确\nschools will be able to effectively intervene ↔\nSchools can boost children's academic performance by effective intervention\nby reducing student absences + These policies assume that missing school for any reason harms\nchildren academically\n↔ boost children's academic performance\nB 项把第二个 assume（学校能有效干预）与前一句的因果（缺课损学业）合起来——\n干预 → 减少缺勤 → 提升学业，是政策预设的完整逻辑，与预判吻合。\nC) 张冠李戴\n原文 hold schools and families accountable for the days children miss 说政策让学\n校和家庭对 \"缺课天数\" 负责，而不是对 out-of-school crises 负责——\"out-of-school\ncrises\" 是 out-of-school challenges 里的概念，C 项把它扣到了 \"政策让学校家庭负责\n的对象\" 头上，移花接木。\nD) 无中生有\n原文从头到尾在讨论 attendance / absences / missed instruction（错过的教学），\n从未讨论 the quality of instruction（教学质量）——D 项凭空引入 \"教学质量\" 概念，\n与原文核心论域无关。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2025-06-1-r-50", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 50, "stem": "What do the authors suggest doing regarding school absences?", "options": ["A) Identifying their underlying causes.", "B) Reframing school attendance policies.", "C) Directing resources to helping needy children.", "D) Pressuring schools to reduce unexcused ones."], "answer": "C", "analysis": "【答案】C （细节题）\n【判型】What do the authors suggest doing regarding school absences? 问的是作者明说的建\n议，原文有原句，不需推断。\n【拆句】结构是 What do the authors suggest doing regarding school absences?——suggest\ndoing 是 suggest 接动名词的固定搭配，What 在动名词 doing 后充当宾语；regarding\n= about，限定话题。结构平铺直叙，无陷阱。\n【定位】We instead suggest using unexcused absence from school as a signal to channel resources to\nthe children and families who need them most.\n我们反而建议：把无故缺勤作为信号，把资源调配给最需要这些资源的孩子和家庭。\n【选项】A) 答非所问\n原文 P13 的建议是 \"把 unexcused absence 当信号 → 把资源调配给需要者\"，不是\nIdentifying their underlying causes（找缺勤的根本原因）——作者已经在前文给出原\n因（家庭/社区危机），末段不再建议 \"找原因\"，而是 \"用信号导资源\"。\nB) 过度推断\n原文 P10–P12 批评了现行政策，但 We instead suggest 的建议内容是 \"用\nunexcused absence 当信号导资源\"，而不是 Reframing school attendance policies\n（重构考勤政策）——作者直接给出替代做法，没把 \"重构政策\" 作为建议本身提出。\nC) ✓ 正确\nchannel resources to ↔ Directing resources to\nthe children and families who need them most ↔ needy children\nC 项把 channel resources to 直接对应 Directing resources to，把 who need them\nmost 压缩为 needy——两步同义改写都是六级高频考法，与预判完全吻合。\nD) 与原文相反\n原文 P12 明确指出现行政策 pressuring schools who serve them to get students to\nschool more often 是负面后果，作者是 \"批评施压\"——D 项 Pressuring schools to\nreduce unexcused ones 反而把 \"施压学校\" 作为作者的建议，与原文立场完全相反。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2025-06-1-r-51", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 51, "stem": "What did the author advise people do for almost a decade?", "options": ["A) Figure out what is the most fascinating job.", "B) Follow widely accepted career counsel.", "C) Pursue their careers with passion.", "D) Do whatever they are zealous for."], "answer": "D", "analysis": "【答案】D （细节题）\n【判型】What did the author advise people do for almost a decade 用 What did 直接问事实，\n没有 infer/suggest/imply/mainly about 等推断或主旨信号。\n【拆句】What did the author advise people do for almost a decade 是 What 引导的特殊疑问\n句，主干 the author advise people do，advise sb. do 处省略 to（口语化结构），for\nalmost a decade 是时间状语修饰整个动作，无陷阱。\n【定位】For almost a decade, I told everyone I encountered that they should do the same. \"Follow\nyour passion,\" I counseled. \"You can figure out the employment stuff later.\"\n在将近十年的时间里，我告诉我遇到的每一个人：他们应该和我做一样的事。我劝告说：\n「追随你的激情」「就业那些事儿你以后再琢磨」。\n【选项】A) 偷换概念\nFigure out what is the most fascinating job 把 fascinated 这个形容作者自己研究方\n向的词嫁接到 job 上，原文 P2 是作者劝别人 follow passion，不是劝别人去找最有趣\n的工作。\nB) 张冠李戴\nFollow widely accepted career counsel 把作者自己当年发出的建议描述为去跟随别人\n的建议，但原文 I counseled 表明作者本人就是 counsel 的发出者。\nC) 偷换概念\nPursue their careers with passion 是『用激情去追求事业』，原文是『追随你的激\n情』，二者并不等价：前者是把激情当工作态度，后者是把激情当工作方向。\nD) ✓ 正确\nFollow your passion ↔ Do whatever they are zealous for\n预判答案是『追随激情/做自己热爱的事』，Do whatever they are zealous for 用\nzealous for 同义替换 passion，正中预判。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2025-06-1-r-52", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 52, "stem": "How did the author feel about the passion principle through his research?", "options": ["A) He was astonished by its consequences.", "B) He was further convinced of its soundness.", "C) He was actually right to follow it through.", "D) He was struck by its broad popularity."], "answer": "A", "analysis": "【答案】A （细节题）\n【判型】How did the author feel about the passion principle through his research 直接问研究\n后的具体感受，是有明确指向的事实题。\n【拆句】How did the author feel about the passion principle through his research 是 How 引\n导的特殊疑问句，主干 the author feel about the passion principle，through his\nresearch 作方式状语，限定感受产生的途径，无陷阱。\n【定位】I was stunned by what I found out about this principle in the research for my new book.\n在为新书做的研究中，我被自己关于这一原则的发现震惊了。\n【选项】A) ✓ 正确\nI was stunned by what I found out ↔ He was astonished by its consequences\n预判是『被研究发现震惊』，astonished by its consequences 把震惊源具体落到\nconsequences 上，与原文 P7 起列举的负面后果一致。\nB) 与原文相反\nHe was further convinced of its soundness 说作者更加确信激情原则的正确性，但原\n文 how problematic it really was 与 stunned 均显示作者发现了它的问题，立场是反\n向的。\nC) 与原文相反\nHe was actually right to follow it through 说作者证实自己当初跟随激情是对的，但\n原文 does not necessarily lead to fulfillment 表明作者得出的是『追随激情未必带来\n满足』的反思结论，并非自我肯定。\nD) 答非所问\nHe was struck by its broad popularity 把震惊对象错挂到『大众接受度』上，但题干\n问的是 through his research，原文 P5 谈大众接受度但用的是中性动词 hold ... in\nhigh regard，作者并未对此 struck。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2025-06-1-r-53", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 53, "stem": "What is important to turning one's passion into a stable, good-paying job?", "options": ["A) Willingness to take unpaid internships and low-paying jobs.", "B) Full academic preparedness and sound career counseling.", "C) Hard work and sacrifice of leisure time.", "D) Financial backing and social connections."], "answer": "D", "analysis": "【答案】D （细节题）\n【判型】What is important to turning one's passion into a stable, good-paying job 问的是某\n事的关键条件，是事实层面的细节题。\n【拆句】What is important to turning one's passion into a stable, good-paying job 中 turning\n... job 是动名词短语作介词 to 的宾语，a stable, good-paying job 中两个形容词并列修饰\njob，无陷阱。\n【定位】Passion-seekers from wealthy families are better able to wait until a job they are passionate\nabout comes along without worrying about student loans in the meantime. They are also\nbetter situated to take unpaid internships to get their foot in the door while their parents pay\ntheir rent. And they often have access to parents' social networks to help them find jobs.\n出身富裕家庭的激情追随者更有能力等到自己真正热爱的工作出现，同时不必担心助学贷\n款；他们也更有条件接受无薪实习以打开职业大门，因为有父母替他们付房租；而且，他们\n往往可以借助父母的社会人脉帮自己找工作。\n【选项】A) 偷换概念\nWillingness to take unpaid internships and low-paying jobs 把『愿意做无薪实习和\n低薪工作』当成转化成功的关键，但原文里无薪实习是富裕者的专利（while their\nparents pay their rent），而低薪非技能岗位则是工薪阶层 end up in low-paying\nunskilled jobs 的不利结局，二者都不是『成功条件』。\nB) 无中生有\nFull academic preparedness and sound career counseling 提到的『学业准备充分』\n在 P8 中根本没有任何线索；P9 虽出现 career counselors 但是被批评的对象、不是关\n键条件。\nC) 张冠李戴\nHard work and sacrifice of leisure time 在原文中是 P10 雇主对激情员工的期待与员\n工实际付出的代价，与题 53 问的『把激情变成稳定高薪』的关键条件无关。\nD) ✓ 正确\nwait until a job they are passionate about comes along without worrying about student loans ↔\nFinancial backing\naccess to parents' social networks to help them find jobs ↔ social connections\n预判要点是经济和人脉两个维度，Financial backing and social connections 一项即覆\n盖两条，是与原文论据结构最完整匹配的概括。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2025-06-1-r-54", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 54, "stem": "What happens when everyone is encouraged to follow their passion?", "options": ["A) Many more career aspirants end up unemployed.", "B) People are less concerned with socioeconomic inequality.", "C) Socioeconomic inequality is likely to persist.", "D) Career counselors are going to lose credibility."], "answer": "C", "analysis": "【答案】C （细节题）\n【判型】What happens when everyone is encouraged to follow their passion 问的是一个已发\n生在原文里的具体结果，不需推断。\n【拆句】What happens when everyone is encouraged to follow their passion 是 What 引导的\n疑问句加 when 时间从句，主干 What happens，everyone is encouraged to follow\ntheir passion 是被动语态从句，无陷阱。\n【定位】Colleges, workplaces and career counselors who promote the \"follow your passion\" path for\neveryone, without leveling the playing field, help perpetuate socioeconomic inequalities\namong career aspirants.\n那些向所有人推行『跟随激情』路径却不去拉平起跑线的高校、用人单位与职业顾问，正在\n加剧职业追求者之间的社会经济不平等。\n【选项】A) 无中生有\nMany more career aspirants end up unemployed 中的『失业』在原文中没有出现，\nP8 只说工薪阶层会落入 low-paying unskilled jobs（低薪非技能岗），那是有工作而\n非失业。\nB) 与原文相反\nPeople are less concerned with socioeconomic inequality 说人们对不平等的关注度\n下降，原文 P9 说的是不平等本身被延续，并不涉及关注度的变化，且 less 反向于作者\n的批评立场。\nC) ✓ 正确\nhelp perpetuate socioeconomic inequalities ↔ Socioeconomic inequality is likely to persist\n预判结果是『不平等被延续』，C 用 persist 同义、用 is likely to 对应 help 的中等程\n度，结构性等值，是 54 的最佳概括。\nD) 过度推断\nCareer counselors are going to lose credibility 推断职业顾问会失去公信力，原文 P9\n只是批评他们推行『跟随激情』而不拉平起跑线，并未说他们会丧失公信力。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2025-06-1-r-55", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 55, "stem": "What does the author say about employers of passionate workers?", "options": ["A) They provide these workers with job stability and a good salary.", "B) They exploit these workers' passion to benefit themselves.", "C) They level the playing field for these workers to reach their goals.", "D) They encourage these workers to realize their aspirations."], "answer": "B", "analysis": "【答案】B （细节题）\n【判型】What does the author say about employers of passionate workers 问的是作者就特定\n对象明示了什么观点，属于事实定位类。\n【拆句】What does the author say about employers of passionate workers 主干 What does\nthe author say about employers，of passionate workers 是 of 短语限定 employers 的\n范围（雇佣激情工作者的雇主），无陷阱。\n【定位】Employers of passionate workers do, too. Potential employers showed greater interest in\npassionate applicants in part because they believed the applicants would work hard at their\njobs without expecting an increase in pay.\n雇用激情员工的雇主也同样受益。潜在雇主对激情型应聘者更感兴趣，部分原因是他们相信\n这些应聘者会卖力工作而不会指望涨薪。\n【选项】A) 与原文相反\nThey provide these workers with job stability and a good salary 说雇主给激情员工\n稳定和高薪，但 They even sacrifice a good salary, job stability 明示员工是在牺牲稳\n定和高薪、不是获得它。\nB) ✓ 正确\nEmployers of passionate workers do, too ↔\nThey exploit these workers' passion to benefit themselves\nwork hard at their jobs without expecting an increase in pay ↔ exploit these workers' passion\n预判定性为『雇主占便宜』，exploit 准确对应『利用他人 passion 为自己获利』，是对\nP10 整段事实最贴近的概括。\nC) 张冠李戴\nThey level the playing field for these workers to reach their goals 把『拉平起跑\n线』归给雇主，但原文 without leveling the playing field 表明这事根本没人做，更不\n是雇主在做。\nD) 偷换概念\nThey encourage these workers to realize their aspirations 用『鼓励员工实现抱负』\n的正面表述粉饰雇主行为，但 P10 揭示的恰是雇主借此让员工 work hard ... without\nexpecting an increase in pay，本质是利用而非成就员工。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "细节题"]}]});
